(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("python",{"python/basics-and-environments":{"html":"\u003cp>Bash is perfect for gluing commands together. Once a script needs data structures, error handling, JSON or an API call, Python is the better tool. Almost every cloud SDK, Ansible module and monitoring plugin you will meet is written in it, so a working knowledge pays off quickly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The small set of Python features that cover most automation work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why every project gets its own virtual environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to install and pin dependencies with pip\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to structure a script so it can be run and imported\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-core-you-actually-use\">The core you actually use\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Python is indentation-sensitive: four spaces define a block. These building blocks show up in nearly every ops script.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"servers = [&quot;web-1&quot;, &quot;web-2&quot;, &quot;db-1&quot;]            # list\nlimits = {&quot;cpu&quot;: 80, &quot;memory&quot;: 90}               # dict\nname, port = &quot;web-1&quot;, 8080                       # tuple unpacking\n\nweb = [s for s in servers if s.startswith(&quot;web&quot;)]   # list comprehension\nprint(f&quot;{name}:{port} has {len(web)} web peers&quot;)    # f-string\n\ndef over_limit(metric: str, value: float) -&gt; bool:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Return True when value is above the configured limit.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    return value &gt; limits.get(metric, 100)\n\nfor metric, value in {&quot;cpu&quot;: 91.5, &quot;memory&quot;: 40}.items():\n    if over_limit(metric, value):\n        print(f&quot;ALERT {metric}={value}&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">servers = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web-1&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web-2&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db-1&quot;\u003c/span>]            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># list\u003c/span>\nlimits = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cpu&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;memory&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">90\u003c/span>}               \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># dict\u003c/span>\nname, port = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web-1&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>                       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># tuple unpacking\u003c/span>\n\nweb = [s \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> s \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> servers \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> s.startswith(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span>)]   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># list comprehension\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{name}\u003c/span>:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{port}\u003c/span> has \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(web)}\u003c/span> web peers&quot;\u003c/span>)    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># f-string\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">over_limit\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">metric: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">str\u003c/span>, value: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">float\u003c/span>\u003c/span>) -&gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">bool\u003c/span>:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;&quot;Return True when value is above the configured limit.&quot;&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> value &gt; limits.get(metric, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">100\u003c/span>)\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> metric, value \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cpu&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">91.5\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;memory&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">40\u003c/span>}.items():\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> over_limit(metric, value):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;ALERT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{metric}\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{value}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Three habits make scripts safer. Use \u003cstrong>f-strings\u003c/strong> for formatting, use \u003ccode>dict.get(key, default)\u003c/code> instead of indexing when a key may be missing, and add short \u003cstrong>type hints\u003c/strong> so the next reader knows what a function expects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"virtual-environments\">Virtual environments\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Python packages install into a Python interpreter. If every project installs into the system Python, two projects needing different versions of the same library will break each other, and an OS upgrade can break your tools. A \u003cstrong>virtual environment\u003c/strong> is a private folder holding its own interpreter links and packages.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python3 -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npython -m pip install --upgrade pip\npython -m pip install requests==2.32.3\npython -m pip freeze &gt; requirements.txt\ndeactivate\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python3 -m venv .venv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">source\u003c/span> .venv/bin/activate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pip install --upgrade pip\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pip install requests==2.32.3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pip freeze &gt; requirements.txt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>deactivate\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Create the environment once per project, activate it in each new shell, and keep \u003ccode>.venv/\u003c/code> out of version control and out of container images built from your code. The \u003ccode>requirements.txt\u003c/code> file records exact versions so a teammate or a pipeline can reproduce the same set:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python3 -m venv .venv &amp;&amp; . .venv/bin/activate\npython -m pip install -r requirements.txt\npython -m pip list --outdated\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python3 -m venv .venv &amp;&amp; . .venv/bin/activate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pip install -r requirements.txt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pip list --outdated\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Always call pip as \u003ccode>python -m pip\u003c/code>. That guarantees the pip belongs to the interpreter you think you are using.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-script-that-can-also-be-imported\">A script that can also be imported\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import sys\n\n\ndef main(argv: list[str]) -&gt; int:\n    if len(argv) != 2:\n        print(&quot;usage: check.py HOSTNAME&quot;, file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n    print(f&quot;checking {argv[1]}&quot;)\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    raise SystemExit(main(sys.argv))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> sys\n\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">main\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">argv: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">list\u003c/span>[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">str\u003c/span>]\u003c/span>) -&gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">int\u003c/span>:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(argv) != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;usage: check.py HOSTNAME&quot;\u003c/span>, file=sys.stderr)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;checking \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{argv[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>]}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> __name__ == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;__main__&quot;\u003c/span>:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> SystemExit(main(sys.argv))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;\u003c/code> guard means the file runs when executed but does nothing when another file imports a function from it. Returning an integer and passing it to \u003ccode>SystemExit\u003c/code> gives the shell a proper exit code, which pipelines rely on.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Python basics and virtual environments fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The core you actually use\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Virtual environments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A script that can also be imported\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The core you actually use to Virtual environments; 2, Virtual environments to A script that can also be imported.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The core you actually use \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Python is indentation-sensitive: four spaces define a block.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using mutable default arguments such as def f(items=[]).\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Virtual environments \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Python packages install into a Python interpreter.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Installing packages into the system Python with sudo pip.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>A script that can also be imported \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot; guard means the file runs when executed but does nothing when another file imports a function from it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgetting to pin versions, so a new release breaks a working script.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-upgrade-that-broke-the-backup-script\">Incident: the upgrade that broke the backup script\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a routine OS update, the nightly backup script fails with \u003ccode>ModuleNotFoundError: No module named &#39;boto3&#39;\u003c/code>. The script ran for two years. Nobody remembers installing boto3, and a quick \u003ccode>pip install\u003c/code> as root makes the error go away until the next update.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The script depended on a package that had been installed into the system Python, and the update replaced that interpreter. The durable fix is to give the script its own environment and record its dependencies:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python3 -m venv /opt/backup/.venv\n/opt/backup/.venv/bin/python -m pip install -r /opt/backup/requirements.txt\n/opt/backup/.venv/bin/python /opt/backup/backup.py\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python3 -m venv /opt/backup/.venv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>/opt/backup/.venv/bin/python -m pip install -r /opt/backup/requirements.txt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>/opt/backup/.venv/bin/python /opt/backup/backup.py\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Call the environment&#39;s interpreter by absolute path in cron or systemd, so no activation step is needed. The script is now independent of the OS Python, and a rebuilt server gets the same versions from \u003ccode>requirements.txt\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Installing packages into the system Python with \u003ccode>sudo pip\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to pin versions, so a new release breaks a working script\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using mutable default arguments such as \u003ccode>def f(items=[])\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Catching every exception with a bare \u003ccode>except:\u003c/code> and hiding the real failure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing tabs and spaces for indentation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-core-you-actually-use","text":"The core you actually use"},{"id":"virtual-environments","text":"Virtual environments"},{"id":"a-script-that-can-also-be-imported","text":"A script that can also be imported"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-upgrade-that-broke-the-backup-script","text":"Incident: the upgrade that broke the backup script"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"The Python tutorial","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/index.html"},{"t":"venv: virtual environments","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/venv.html"},{"t":"pip documentation","u":"https://pip.pypa.io/en/stable/"}],"text":"Bash is perfect for gluing commands together. Once a script needs data structures, error handling, JSON or an API call, Python is the better tool. Almost every cloud SDK, Ansible module and monitoring plugin you will meet is written in it, so a working knowledge pays off quickly. What you will learn The small set of Python features that cover most automation work Why every project gets its own virtual environment How to install and pin dependencies with pip How to structure a script so it can be run and imported The core you actually use Python is indentation-sensitive: four spaces define a block. These building blocks show up in nearly every ops script. servers = [ \"web-1\" , \"web-2\" , \"db-1\" ] # list limits = { \"cpu\" : 80 , \"memory\" : 90 } # dict name, port = \"web-1\" , 8080 # tuple unpacking web = [s for s in servers if s.startswith( \"web\" )] # list comprehension print ( f\" {name} : {port} has { len (web)} web peers\" ) # f-string def over_limit ( metric: str , value: float ) -> bool : \"\"\"Return True when value is above the configured limit.\"\"\" return value > limits.get(metric, 100 ) for metric, value in { \"cpu\" : 91.5 , \"memory\" : 40 }.items(): if over_limit(metric, value): print ( f\"ALERT {metric} = {value} \" ) Three habits make scripts safer. Use f-strings for formatting, use dict.get(key, default) instead of indexing when a key may be missing, and add short type hints so the next reader knows what a function expects. Virtual environments Python packages install into a Python interpreter. If every project installs into the system Python, two projects needing different versions of the same library will break each other, and an OS upgrade can break your tools. A virtual environment is a private folder holding its own interpreter links and packages. $ python3 -m venv .venv $ source .venv/bin/activate $ python -m pip install --upgrade pip $ python -m pip install requests==2.32.3 $ python -m pip freeze > requirements.txt $ deactivate Create the environment once per project, activate it in each new shell, and keep .venv/ out of version control and out of container images built from your code. The requirements.txt file records exact versions so a teammate or a pipeline can reproduce the same set: $ python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate $ python -m pip install -r requirements.txt $ python -m pip list --outdated Always call pip as python -m pip . That guarantees the pip belongs to the interpreter you think you are using. A script that can also be imported import sys def main ( argv: list [ str ] ) -> int : if len (argv) != 2 : print ( \"usage: check.py HOSTNAME\" , file=sys.stderr) return 2 print ( f\"checking {argv[ 1 ]} \" ) return 0 if __name__ == \"__main__\" : raise SystemExit(main(sys.argv)) The if __name__ == \"__main__\" guard means the file runs when executed but does nothing when another file imports a function from it. Returning an integer and passing it to SystemExit gives the shell a proper exit code, which pipelines rely on. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Python basics and virtual environments fits together Flow in order: 1, The core you actually use to Virtual environments; 2, Virtual environments to A script that can also be imported. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. The core you actually use Step What it does: Python is indentation-sensitive: four spaces define a block. If it fails or is weak: Using mutable default arguments such as def f(items=[]). Virtual environments Step What it does: Python packages install into a Python interpreter. If it fails or is weak: Installing packages into the system Python with sudo pip. A script that can also be imported Step What it does: The if __name__ == \"__main__\" guard means the file runs when executed but does nothing when anoth"},"python/files-and-processes":{"html":"\u003cp>Most automation boils down to three jobs: read or write files, run another program, and decide what to do when something fails. Python&#39;s standard library covers all three without installing anything.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading and writing files with \u003ccode>pathlib\u003c/code> and context managers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running commands with \u003ccode>subprocess.run\u003c/code> safely and checking the result\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handling exceptions deliberately and exiting with useful codes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging instead of scattering \u003ccode>print\u003c/code> calls\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"files-with-pathlib\">Files with pathlib\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>pathlib.Path\u003c/code> treats paths as objects, so you avoid string concatenation and platform differences.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"from pathlib import Path\n\nconfig = Path(&quot;/etc/myapp/app.conf&quot;)\nif not config.exists():\n    raise SystemExit(f&quot;missing config: {config}&quot;)\n\ntext = config.read_text(encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;)\nlines = [l.strip() for l in text.splitlines() if l and not l.startswith(&quot;#&quot;)]\n\nout = Path(&quot;/var/tmp/report.txt&quot;)\nout.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\nwith out.open(&quot;w&quot;, encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;) as fh:\n    for line in lines:\n        fh.write(line + &quot;\\n&quot;)\n\nfor log in Path(&quot;/var/log/myapp&quot;).glob(&quot;*.log&quot;):\n    print(log.name, log.stat().st_size)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> pathlib \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> Path\n\nconfig = Path(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/etc/myapp/app.conf&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> config.exists():\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> SystemExit(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;missing config: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{config}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\ntext = config.read_text(encoding=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;utf-8&quot;\u003c/span>)\nlines = [l.strip() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> l \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> text.splitlines() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> l \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> l.startswith(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;#&quot;\u003c/span>)]\n\nout = Path(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/var/tmp/report.txt&quot;\u003c/span>)\nout.parent.mkdir(parents=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>, exist_ok=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> out.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">open\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;w&quot;\u003c/span>, encoding=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;utf-8&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> fh:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> line \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> lines:\n        fh.write(line + \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\\n&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> log \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> Path(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/var/log/myapp&quot;\u003c/span>).glob(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*.log&quot;\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(log.name, log.stat().st_size)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>with\u003c/code> block closes the file even when an error occurs. For configuration files in JSON or YAML, parse them with \u003ccode>json.loads\u003c/code> or a YAML library instead of splitting lines yourself.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"running-programs\">Running programs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>subprocess.run\u003c/code> is the safe default. Pass the command as a \u003cstrong>list\u003c/strong>, not a string, so the shell never interprets it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import subprocess\n\nresult = subprocess.run(\n    [&quot;systemctl&quot;, &quot;is-active&quot;, &quot;nginx&quot;],\n    capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10,\n)\nprint(result.returncode, result.stdout.strip())\n\n# fail loudly when a command must succeed\nsubprocess.run([&quot;tar&quot;, &quot;-czf&quot;, &quot;/tmp/site.tgz&quot;, &quot;/var/www&quot;], check=True)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> subprocess\n\nresult = subprocess.run(\n    [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;systemctl&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;is-active&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;nginx&quot;\u003c/span>],\n    capture_output=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>, text=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>, timeout=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>,\n)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(result.returncode, result.stdout.strip())\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># fail loudly when a command must succeed\u003c/span>\nsubprocess.run([\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tar&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;-czf&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/tmp/site.tgz&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/var/www&quot;\u003c/span>], check=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>check=True\u003c/code> raises \u003ccode>CalledProcessError\u003c/code> on a non-zero exit. \u003ccode>timeout\u003c/code> stops a hung command from hanging your script. Avoid \u003ccode>shell=True\u003c/code> with any value that comes from a user, a file or an API, because it enables command injection.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exceptions-you-expect\">Exceptions you expect\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Catch the specific errors you can handle and let everything else stop the script with a traceback.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import subprocess\nimport sys\n\ndef restart(service: str) -&gt; bool:\n    try:\n        subprocess.run([&quot;systemctl&quot;, &quot;restart&quot;, service], check=True, timeout=30)\n    except subprocess.CalledProcessError as exc:\n        print(f&quot;restart failed, exit code {exc.returncode}&quot;, file=sys.stderr)\n        return False\n    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        print(&quot;restart timed out&quot;, file=sys.stderr)\n        return False\n    return True\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> subprocess\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> sys\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">restart\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">service: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">str\u003c/span>\u003c/span>) -&gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">bool\u003c/span>:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">try\u003c/span>:\n        subprocess.run([\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;systemctl&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;restart&quot;\u003c/span>, service], check=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>, timeout=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> subprocess.CalledProcessError \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> exc:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;restart failed, exit code \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{exc.returncode}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>, file=sys.stderr)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;restart timed out&quot;\u003c/span>, file=sys.stderr)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"logging\">Logging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>logging\u003c/code> gives timestamps and levels, and can be redirected to files or the journal without changing the code.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(\n    level=logging.INFO,\n    format=&quot;%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(message)s&quot;,\n)\nlog = logging.getLogger(&quot;cleanup&quot;)\nlog.info(&quot;removing files older than %d days&quot;, 14)\nlog.warning(&quot;directory %s is almost full&quot;, &quot;/var/tmp&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(\n    level=logging.INFO,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">format\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(message)s&quot;\u003c/span>,\n)\nlog = logging.getLogger(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cleanup&quot;\u003c/span>)\nlog.info(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;removing files older than %d days&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">14\u003c/span>)\nlog.warning(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;directory %s is almost full&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/var/tmp&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>log.exception(&quot;message&quot;)\u003c/code> inside an \u003ccode>except\u003c/code> block to record the traceback.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python3 cleanup.py\necho $?\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python3 cleanup.py\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2025-06-02 03:00:01,114 INFO removing files older than 14 days\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2025-06-02 03:00:01,201 WARNING directory /var/tmp is almost full\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> $?\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Files, processes and error handling fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Files with pathlib\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Running programs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Exceptions you expect\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Logging\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Files with pathlib to Running programs; 2, Running programs to Exceptions you expect; 3, Exceptions you expect to Logging.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Files with pathlib \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> pathlib.Path treats paths as objects, so you avoid string concatenation and platform differences.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using print for everything instead of logging with levels.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Running programs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> subprocess.run is the safe default. Pass the command as a list, not a string, so the shell never interprets it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using shell=True with interpolated values.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Exceptions you expect \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Catch the specific errors you can handle and let everything else stop the script with a traceback.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Swallowing exceptions so the script reports success when it failed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Logging \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> logging gives timestamps and levels, and can be redirected to files or the journal without changing the code.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Opening files without a context manager, leaving them unclosed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-cleanup-that-deleted-too-much\">Incident: the cleanup that deleted too much\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cleanup script runs \u003ccode>subprocess.run(f&quot;rm -rf {base}/{name}&quot;, shell=True)\u003c/code>. One night \u003ccode>name\u003c/code> comes back empty from a config file, and the command becomes \u003ccode>rm -rf /data/\u003c/code>. Half of the shared volume is deleted before anyone notices.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Three defects combined: a shell string, an unvalidated value and no dry run. The safer version validates input, uses \u003ccode>pathlib\u003c/code> and refuses to act on anything outside the intended directory:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import shutil\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nBASE = Path(&quot;/data/cache&quot;).resolve()\n\ndef remove(name: str, dry_run: bool = True) -&gt; None:\n    if not name or &quot;/&quot; in name:\n        raise ValueError(f&quot;refusing unsafe name: {name!r}&quot;)\n    target = (BASE / name).resolve()\n    if BASE not in target.parents:\n        raise ValueError(f&quot;{target} is outside {BASE}&quot;)\n    print((&quot;would remove &quot; if dry_run else &quot;removing &quot;) + str(target))\n    if not dry_run:\n        shutil.rmtree(target)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> shutil\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> pathlib \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> Path\n\nBASE = Path(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/data/cache&quot;\u003c/span>).resolve()\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">remove\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">name: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">str\u003c/span>, dry_run: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">bool\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>\u003c/span>) -&gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">or\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> name:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> ValueError(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;refusing unsafe name: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{name!r}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    target = (BASE / name).resolve()\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> BASE \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> target.parents:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> ValueError(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{target}\u003c/span> is outside \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{BASE}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>((\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;would remove &quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> dry_run \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;removing &quot;\u003c/span>) + \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">str\u003c/span>(target))\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> dry_run:\n        shutil.rmtree(target)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Default to a dry run, print what would happen, and require an explicit flag for the destructive step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"predict-the-result\">Predict the result\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> What happens when this runs and ls fails with a non-zero exit code?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>import subprocess\nsubprocess.run([&quot;ls&quot;, &quot;/missing&quot;], check=True)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> It raises subprocess.CalledProcessError.\u003c/p>\u003cp>check=True turns a non-zero exit code into an exception. Without it the code continues silently with the failure in returncode.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A file is opened with the with statement. What is guaranteed when the block ends, even after an error?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>with open(&quot;out.txt&quot;, &quot;w&quot;) as f:\n    f.write(&quot;hello&quot;)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The file is closed and the data is flushed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The context manager calls close on exit, including when an exception happens. This avoids leaked handles.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>shell=True\u003c/code> with interpolated values\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Opening files without a context manager, leaving them unclosed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting \u003ccode>encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;\u003c/code>, which breaks on non-English systems\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>print\u003c/code> for everything instead of logging with levels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Swallowing exceptions so the script reports success when it failed\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"files-with-pathlib","text":"Files with pathlib"},{"id":"running-programs","text":"Running programs"},{"id":"exceptions-you-expect","text":"Exceptions you expect"},{"id":"logging","text":"Logging"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-cleanup-that-deleted-too-much","text":"Incident: the cleanup that deleted too much"},{"id":"predict-the-result","text":"Predict the result"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"pathlib module","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/pathlib.html"},{"t":"subprocess module","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html"},{"t":"Logging HOWTO","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/howto/logging.html"}],"text":"Most automation boils down to three jobs: read or write files, run another program, and decide what to do when something fails. Python&#39;s standard library covers all three without installing anything. What you will learn Reading and writing files with pathlib and context managers Running commands with subprocess.run safely and checking the result Handling exceptions deliberately and exiting with useful codes Logging instead of scattering print calls Files with pathlib pathlib.Path treats paths as objects, so you avoid string concatenation and platform differences. from pathlib import Path config = Path( \"/etc/myapp/app.conf\" ) if not config.exists(): raise SystemExit( f\"missing config: {config} \" ) text = config.read_text(encoding= \"utf-8\" ) lines = [l.strip() for l in text.splitlines() if l and not l.startswith( \"#\" )] out = Path( \"/var/tmp/report.txt\" ) out.parent.mkdir(parents= True , exist_ok= True ) with out. open ( \"w\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as fh: for line in lines: fh.write(line + \"\\n\" ) for log in Path( \"/var/log/myapp\" ).glob( \"*.log\" ): print (log.name, log.stat().st_size) The with block closes the file even when an error occurs. For configuration files in JSON or YAML, parse them with json.loads or a YAML library instead of splitting lines yourself. Running programs subprocess.run is the safe default. Pass the command as a list , not a string, so the shell never interprets it. import subprocess result = subprocess.run( [ \"systemctl\" , \"is-active\" , \"nginx\" ], capture_output= True , text= True , timeout= 10 , ) print (result.returncode, result.stdout.strip()) # fail loudly when a command must succeed subprocess.run([ \"tar\" , \"-czf\" , \"/tmp/site.tgz\" , \"/var/www\" ], check= True ) check=True raises CalledProcessError on a non-zero exit. timeout stops a hung command from hanging your script. Avoid shell=True with any value that comes from a user, a file or an API, because it enables command injection. Exceptions you expect Catch the specific errors you can handle and let everything else stop the script with a traceback. import subprocess import sys def restart ( service: str ) -> bool : try : subprocess.run([ \"systemctl\" , \"restart\" , service], check= True , timeout= 30 ) except subprocess.CalledProcessError as exc: print ( f\"restart failed, exit code {exc.returncode} \" , file=sys.stderr) return False except subprocess.TimeoutExpired: print ( \"restart timed out\" , file=sys.stderr) return False return True Logging logging gives timestamps and levels, and can be redirected to files or the journal without changing the code. import logging logging.basicConfig( level=logging.INFO, format = \"%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(message)s\" , ) log = logging.getLogger( \"cleanup\" ) log.info( \"removing files older than %d days\" , 14 ) log.warning( \"directory %s is almost full\" , \"/var/tmp\" ) Use log.exception(\"message\") inside an except block to record the traceback. $ python3 cleanup.py 2025-06-02 03:00:01,114 INFO removing files older than 14 days 2025-06-02 03:00:01,201 WARNING directory /var/tmp is almost full $ echo $? 0 How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Files, processes and error handling fits together Flow in order: 1, Files with pathlib to Running programs; 2, Running programs to Exceptions you expect; 3, Exceptions you expect to Logging. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Files with pathlib Step What it does: pathlib.Path treats paths as objects, so you avoid string concatenation and platform differences. If it fails or is weak: Using print for everything instead of logging with levels. Running programs Step What it does: subprocess.run is the safe default. Pass the command as a list, not a string, so the shell never interprets it. If it fails or is weak: Using shell=Tr"},"python/http-apis-and-json":{"html":"\u003cp>Cloud providers, ticketing systems, chat tools and monitoring platforms all expose REST APIs. Calling them from Python turns a repetitive console task into a script you can schedule and review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Making GET and POST requests with the \u003ccode>requests\u003c/code> library\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Working with JSON in both directions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Timeouts, status codes, retries and pagination\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading secrets from the environment\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-first-request\">A first request\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import requests\n\nresp = requests.get(\n    &quot;https://api.example.com/v1/servers&quot;,\n    headers={&quot;Accept&quot;: &quot;application/json&quot;},\n    params={&quot;status&quot;: &quot;running&quot;, &quot;limit&quot;: 50},\n    timeout=10,\n)\nresp.raise_for_status()\nfor server in resp.json()[&quot;items&quot;]:\n    print(server[&quot;id&quot;], server[&quot;name&quot;])\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> requests\n\nresp = requests.get(\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://api.example.com/v1/servers&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    headers={\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Accept&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;application/json&quot;\u003c/span>},\n    params={\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;running&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;limit&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">50\u003c/span>},\n    timeout=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>,\n)\nresp.raise_for_status()\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> server \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> resp.json()[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;items&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(server[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;\u003c/span>], server[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Three details matter. \u003cstrong>Always set a timeout\u003c/strong>: without one, \u003ccode>requests\u003c/code> can wait forever. \u003cstrong>Call \u003ccode>raise_for_status()\u003c/code>\u003c/strong> so 4xx and 5xx responses become exceptions instead of silently parsed error pages. \u003cstrong>Use \u003ccode>params=\u003c/code>\u003c/strong> rather than building query strings by hand, because it handles encoding.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sending-json\">Sending JSON\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import os\nimport requests\n\ntoken = os.environ[&quot;API_TOKEN&quot;]          # never hard-code tokens\npayload = {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;web-3&quot;, &quot;size&quot;: &quot;small&quot;, &quot;tags&quot;: [&quot;prod&quot;, &quot;web&quot;]}\n\nresp = requests.post(\n    &quot;https://api.example.com/v1/servers&quot;,\n    json=payload,                         # sets the header and encodes for you\n    headers={&quot;Authorization&quot;: f&quot;Bearer {token}&quot;},\n    timeout=10,\n)\nif resp.status_code == 409:\n    print(&quot;already exists&quot;)\nelse:\n    resp.raise_for_status()\n    print(&quot;created&quot;, resp.json()[&quot;id&quot;])\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> os\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> requests\n\ntoken = os.environ[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;API_TOKEN&quot;\u003c/span>]          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># never hard-code tokens\u003c/span>\npayload = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web-3&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;size&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;small&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tags&quot;\u003c/span>: [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span>]}\n\nresp = requests.post(\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://api.example.com/v1/servers&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    json=payload,                         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># sets the header and encodes for you\u003c/span>\n    headers={\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;Bearer \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{token}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>},\n    timeout=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>,\n)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> resp.status_code == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">409\u003c/span>:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;already exists&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span>:\n    resp.raise_for_status()\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;created&quot;\u003c/span>, resp.json()[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;\u003c/span>])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Reading \u003ccode>os.environ[&quot;API_TOKEN&quot;]\u003c/code> fails immediately with a \u003ccode>KeyError\u003c/code> if the variable is missing, which is better than sending an empty header. Never commit tokens, print them or include them in error messages.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pagination-and-retries\">Pagination and retries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>APIs return data in pages. A reusable session with retries handles brief outages and rate limits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import requests\nfrom requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter\nfrom urllib3.util.retry import Retry\n\ndef make_session() -&gt; requests.Session:\n    retry = Retry(total=4, backoff_factor=0.5,\n                  status_forcelist=(429, 500, 502, 503, 504),\n                  allowed_methods=(&quot;GET&quot;, &quot;PUT&quot;, &quot;DELETE&quot;))\n    s = requests.Session()\n    s.mount(&quot;https://&quot;, HTTPAdapter(max_retries=retry))\n    return s\n\ndef all_servers(session: requests.Session):\n    url = &quot;https://api.example.com/v1/servers&quot;\n    while url:\n        r = session.get(url, timeout=10)\n        r.raise_for_status()\n        body = r.json()\n        yield from body[&quot;items&quot;]\n        url = body.get(&quot;next&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> requests\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> requests.adapters \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> HTTPAdapter\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> urllib3.util.retry \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> Retry\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">make_session\u003c/span>() -&gt; requests.Session:\n    retry = Retry(total=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>, backoff_factor=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.5\u003c/span>,\n                  status_forcelist=(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">429\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">500\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">502\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">503\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">504\u003c/span>),\n                  allowed_methods=(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;GET&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;PUT&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DELETE&quot;\u003c/span>))\n    s = requests.Session()\n    s.mount(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://&quot;\u003c/span>, HTTPAdapter(max_retries=retry))\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> s\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">all_servers\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">session: requests.Session\u003c/span>):\n    url = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://api.example.com/v1/servers&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">while\u003c/span> url:\n        r = session.get(url, timeout=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>)\n        r.raise_for_status()\n        body = r.json()\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">yield\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> body[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;items&quot;\u003c/span>]\n        url = body.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;next&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Do not retry non-idempotent \u003ccode>POST\u003c/code> requests blindly: a retry after a timeout could create a duplicate. Honour \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code> headers and use exponential backoff so you do not hammer a service that is already struggling.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"json-basics\">JSON basics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import json\n\nraw = '{&quot;name&quot;: &quot;web-1&quot;, &quot;cpu&quot;: 0.42, &quot;tags&quot;: [&quot;prod&quot;]}'\ndata = json.loads(raw)                  # string -&gt; dict\ndata[&quot;cpu&quot;] += 0.1\nprint(json.dumps(data, indent=2, sort_keys=True))\n\nwith open(&quot;servers.json&quot;, &quot;w&quot;, encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;) as fh:\n    json.dump(data, fh, indent=2)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> json\n\nraw = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;name&quot;: &quot;web-1&quot;, &quot;cpu&quot;: 0.42, &quot;tags&quot;: [&quot;prod&quot;]}&#x27;\u003c/span>\ndata = json.loads(raw)                  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># string -&gt; dict\u003c/span>\ndata[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cpu&quot;\u003c/span>] += \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(json.dumps(data, indent=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>, sort_keys=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>))\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">open\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;servers.json&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;w&quot;\u003c/span>, encoding=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;utf-8&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> fh:\n    json.dump(data, fh, indent=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python3 list_servers.py | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python3 list_servers.py | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">srv-001 web-1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">srv-002 web-2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">srv-003 db-1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-script-that-hung-the-deployment\">Incident: the script that hung the deployment\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deployment step calls a status API with \u003ccode>requests.get(url)\u003c/code>. The API&#39;s load balancer stalls, the connection never closes, and the pipeline job hangs for six hours until a runner timeout kills it. The release is delayed and the runner pool is exhausted.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>requests\u003c/code> has no default timeout. The fix is a single argument, and a retry policy for the transient case:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resp = requests.get(url, timeout=(3.05, 10))   # connect timeout, read timeout\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">resp = requests.get(url, timeout=(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3.05\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>))   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># connect timeout, read timeout\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Wrap the call in the session from the previous section so a short outage is retried a few times with backoff, then fails the job quickly with a clear error. A fast failure with a message is always better than a silent hang.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"build&gt;send;send&gt;status;send&gt;parse;status&gt;out;parse&gt;out\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A reliable API call from a script\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"build\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Build the request\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"send\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Send with retries\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"status\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Check the status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"parse\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Parse JSON\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"out\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Use or log the result\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Build the request to Send with retries; 2, Send with retries to Check the status; 3, Send with retries to Parse JSON; 4, Check the status to Use or log the result; 5, Parse JSON to Use or log the result.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"build\">\u003cdt>Build the request \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> URL, headers, token from the environment and a timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No timeout means a hung server hangs your script forever.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"send\">\u003cdt>Send with retries \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A session retries on 429 and 5xx with backoff.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retrying non-idempotent calls can create duplicates.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"status\">\u003cdt>Check the status \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Call raise_for_status so 4xx and 5xx become errors.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring status parses an error page as data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"parse\">\u003cdt>Parse JSON \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Use response.json() and read fields with get.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A missing key raises KeyError or silently gives None.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"out\">\u003cdt>Use or log the result \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Return data or log the failure with the request id.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without the id you cannot find the matching server log.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Omitting \u003ccode>timeout\u003c/code>, which allows indefinite hangs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Parsing the body without checking the status code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building URLs and query strings with string concatenation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging full request headers, which leaks tokens\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying \u003ccode>POST\u003c/code> requests that are not idempotent\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-first-request","text":"A first request"},{"id":"sending-json","text":"Sending JSON"},{"id":"pagination-and-retries","text":"Pagination and retries"},{"id":"json-basics","text":"JSON basics"},{"id":"incident-the-script-that-hung-the-deployment","text":"Incident: the script that hung the deployment"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Requests documentation","u":"https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/"},{"t":"json module","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html"},{"t":"RFC 9110: HTTP semantics","u":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110"}],"text":"Cloud providers, ticketing systems, chat tools and monitoring platforms all expose REST APIs. Calling them from Python turns a repetitive console task into a script you can schedule and review. What you will learn Making GET and POST requests with the requests library Working with JSON in both directions Timeouts, status codes, retries and pagination Reading secrets from the environment A first request import requests resp = requests.get( \"https://api.example.com/v1/servers\" , headers={ \"Accept\" : \"application/json\" }, params={ \"status\" : \"running\" , \"limit\" : 50 }, timeout= 10 , ) resp.raise_for_status() for server in resp.json()[ \"items\" ]: print (server[ \"id\" ], server[ \"name\" ]) Three details matter. Always set a timeout : without one, requests can wait forever. Call raise_for_status() so 4xx and 5xx responses become exceptions instead of silently parsed error pages. Use params= rather than building query strings by hand, because it handles encoding. Sending JSON import os import requests token = os.environ[ \"API_TOKEN\" ] # never hard-code tokens payload = { \"name\" : \"web-3\" , \"size\" : \"small\" , \"tags\" : [ \"prod\" , \"web\" ]} resp = requests.post( \"https://api.example.com/v1/servers\" , json=payload, # sets the header and encodes for you headers={ \"Authorization\" : f\"Bearer {token} \" }, timeout= 10 , ) if resp.status_code == 409 : print ( \"already exists\" ) else : resp.raise_for_status() print ( \"created\" , resp.json()[ \"id\" ]) Reading os.environ[\"API_TOKEN\"] fails immediately with a KeyError if the variable is missing, which is better than sending an empty header. Never commit tokens, print them or include them in error messages. Pagination and retries APIs return data in pages. A reusable session with retries handles brief outages and rate limits. import requests from requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter from urllib3.util.retry import Retry def make_session () -> requests.Session: retry = Retry(total= 4 , backoff_factor= 0.5 , status_forcelist=( 429 , 500 , 502 , 503 , 504 ), allowed_methods=( \"GET\" , \"PUT\" , \"DELETE\" )) s = requests.Session() s.mount( \"https://\" , HTTPAdapter(max_retries=retry)) return s def all_servers ( session: requests.Session ): url = \"https://api.example.com/v1/servers\" while url: r = session.get(url, timeout= 10 ) r.raise_for_status() body = r.json() yield from body[ \"items\" ] url = body.get( \"next\" ) Do not retry non-idempotent POST requests blindly: a retry after a timeout could create a duplicate. Honour Retry-After headers and use exponential backoff so you do not hammer a service that is already struggling. JSON basics import json raw = &#x27;{\"name\": \"web-1\", \"cpu\": 0.42, \"tags\": [\"prod\"]}&#x27; data = json.loads(raw) # string -> dict data[ \"cpu\" ] += 0.1 print (json.dumps(data, indent= 2 , sort_keys= True )) with open ( \"servers.json\" , \"w\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as fh: json.dump(data, fh, indent= 2 ) $ python3 list_servers.py | head -3 srv-001 web-1 srv-002 web-2 srv-003 db-1 Incident: the script that hung the deployment Incident A deployment step calls a status API with requests.get(url) . The API&#39;s load balancer stalls, the connection never closes, and the pipeline job hangs for six hours until a runner timeout kills it. The release is delayed and the runner pool is exhausted. requests has no default timeout. The fix is a single argument, and a retry policy for the transient case: resp = requests.get(url, timeout=( 3.05 , 10 )) # connect timeout, read timeout Wrap the call in the session from the previous section so a short outage is retried a few times with backoff, then fails the job quickly with a clear error. A fast failure with a message is always better than a silent hang. A reliable API call from a script Flow in order: 1, Build the request to Send with retries; 2, Send with retries to Check the status; 3, Send with retries to Parse JSON; 4, Check the status to Use or log the result; 5, Parse JSON to Use or log the result. Step Queue Observability Numbered arrows show the order o"},"python/cloud-sdks":{"html":"\u003cp>The cloud consoles and CLIs are fine for exploring, but repeatable work belongs in code. Both AWS and Azure publish official Python SDKs, and the patterns you learn for one transfer directly to the other.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Authenticating with roles and default credential chains\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Listing and filtering resources with boto3, including pagination\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The same tasks with the Azure SDK\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing scripts that are safe to run twice\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"authentication-first\">Authentication first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Never put access keys in a script. On a developer machine use SSO or a profile. On a server or pipeline use an \u003cstrong>instance role\u003c/strong>, a \u003cstrong>workload identity\u003c/strong> or an assumed role, so the SDK finds short-lived credentials by itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws sso login --profile dev\nexport AWS_PROFILE=dev\npython3 -c &quot;import boto3; print(boto3.client('sts').get_caller_identity()['Arn'])&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws sso login --profile dev\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> AWS_PROFILE=dev\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python3 -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;import boto3; print(boto3.client(&#x27;sts&#x27;).get_caller_identity()[&#x27;Arn&#x27;])&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/Developer/reddy\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Always print the identity first in any script that changes things. Running the right code against the wrong account is a classic way to cause an outage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"boto3-essentials\">boto3 essentials\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import boto3\n\nec2 = boto3.client(&quot;ec2&quot;, region_name=&quot;eu-west-1&quot;)\n\npaginator = ec2.get_paginator(&quot;describe_instances&quot;)\npages = paginator.paginate(\n    Filters=[{&quot;Name&quot;: &quot;tag:Env&quot;, &quot;Values&quot;: [&quot;prod&quot;]},\n             {&quot;Name&quot;: &quot;instance-state-name&quot;, &quot;Values&quot;: [&quot;running&quot;]}]\n)\nfor page in pages:\n    for reservation in page[&quot;Reservations&quot;]:\n        for inst in reservation[&quot;Instances&quot;]:\n            name = next((t[&quot;Value&quot;] for t in inst.get(&quot;Tags&quot;, []) if t[&quot;Key&quot;] == &quot;Name&quot;), &quot;-&quot;)\n            print(inst[&quot;InstanceId&quot;], inst[&quot;InstanceType&quot;], name)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> boto3\n\nec2 = boto3.client(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ec2&quot;\u003c/span>, region_name=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\npaginator = ec2.get_paginator(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;describe_instances&quot;\u003c/span>)\npages = paginator.paginate(\n    Filters=[{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tag:Env&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Values&quot;\u003c/span>: [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod&quot;\u003c/span>]},\n             {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;instance-state-name&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Values&quot;\u003c/span>: [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;running&quot;\u003c/span>]}]\n)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> page \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> pages:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> reservation \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> page[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Reservations&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> inst \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> reservation[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Instances&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n            name = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">next\u003c/span>((t[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Value&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> t \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> inst.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Tags&quot;\u003c/span>, []) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> t[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Key&quot;\u003c/span>] == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Name&quot;\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;-&quot;\u003c/span>)\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(inst[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;InstanceId&quot;\u003c/span>], inst[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;InstanceType&quot;\u003c/span>], name)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>API calls return at most one page of results. A \u003cstrong>paginator\u003c/strong> hides the continuation tokens; without it you silently process only the first page. Filter on the server with \u003ccode>Filters=\u003c/code> instead of downloading everything.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-safe-repeatable-task\">A safe, repeatable task\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scripts that change infrastructure should be \u003cstrong>idempotent\u003c/strong>: running them twice has the same result as once.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import boto3\nfrom botocore.exceptions import ClientError\n\ns3 = boto3.client(&quot;s3&quot;)\n\ndef ensure_bucket(name: str, region: str) -&gt; None:\n    try:\n        s3.head_bucket(Bucket=name)\n        print(&quot;exists:&quot;, name)\n    except ClientError as exc:\n        if exc.response[&quot;Error&quot;][&quot;Code&quot;] not in (&quot;404&quot;, &quot;NoSuchBucket&quot;):\n            raise\n        s3.create_bucket(Bucket=name,\n                         CreateBucketConfiguration={&quot;LocationConstraint&quot;: region})\n        s3.put_public_access_block(\n            Bucket=name,\n            PublicAccessBlockConfiguration={\n                &quot;BlockPublicAcls&quot;: True, &quot;IgnorePublicAcls&quot;: True,\n                &quot;BlockPublicPolicy&quot;: True, &quot;RestrictPublicBuckets&quot;: True})\n        print(&quot;created:&quot;, name)\n\nensure_bucket(&quot;acme-reports-eu&quot;, &quot;eu-west-1&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> boto3\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> botocore.exceptions \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> ClientError\n\ns3 = boto3.client(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;s3&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">ensure_bucket\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">name: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">str\u003c/span>, region: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">str\u003c/span>\u003c/span>) -&gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">try\u003c/span>:\n        s3.head_bucket(Bucket=name)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;exists:&quot;\u003c/span>, name)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> ClientError \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> exc:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> exc.response[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Error&quot;\u003c/span>][\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Code&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;404&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;NoSuchBucket&quot;\u003c/span>):\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span>\n        s3.create_bucket(Bucket=name,\n                         CreateBucketConfiguration={\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;LocationConstraint&quot;\u003c/span>: region})\n        s3.put_public_access_block(\n            Bucket=name,\n            PublicAccessBlockConfiguration={\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;BlockPublicAcls&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;IgnorePublicAcls&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>,\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;BlockPublicPolicy&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;RestrictPublicBuckets&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>})\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;created:&quot;\u003c/span>, name)\n\nensure_bucket(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;acme-reports-eu&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Check first, create only if missing, and apply the secure settings in the same script.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-azure-equivalent\">The Azure equivalent\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"from azure.identity import DefaultAzureCredential\nfrom azure.mgmt.compute import ComputeManagementClient\n\ncred = DefaultAzureCredential()          # CLI login, managed identity, or env vars\ncompute = ComputeManagementClient(cred, subscription_id=&quot;00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000&quot;)\n\nfor vm in compute.virtual_machines.list_all():\n    print(vm.name, vm.location, vm.hardware_profile.vm_size)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> azure.identity \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> DefaultAzureCredential\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> azure.mgmt.compute \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> ComputeManagementClient\n\ncred = DefaultAzureCredential()          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># CLI login, managed identity, or env vars\u003c/span>\ncompute = ComputeManagementClient(cred, subscription_id=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> vm \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> compute.virtual_machines.list_all():\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(vm.name, vm.location, vm.hardware_profile.vm_size)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>DefaultAzureCredential\u003c/code> tries several sources in order, so the same code works on a laptop after \u003ccode>az login\u003c/code> and in Azure with a managed identity. Install the packages in your virtual environment:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python -m pip install boto3 azure-identity azure-mgmt-compute\npython -m pip freeze | grep -E &quot;boto3|azure&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pip install boto3 azure-identity azure-mgmt-compute\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pip freeze | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;boto3|azure&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Cloud SDKs: boto3 and the Azure SDK fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Authentication first\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">boto3 essentials\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A safe, repeatable task\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The Azure equivalent\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Authentication first to boto3 essentials; 2, boto3 essentials to A safe, repeatable task; 3, A safe, repeatable task to The Azure equivalent.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Authentication first \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Never put access keys in a script. On a developer machine use SSO or a profile.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hard-coding access keys in scripts or committing them.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>boto3 essentials \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> API calls return at most one page of results.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring pagination and processing only the first page.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>A safe, repeatable task \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Scripts that change infrastructure should be idempotent: running them twice has the same result as once.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Writing scripts that fail when a resource already exists.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>The Azure equivalent \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> DefaultAzureCredential tries several sources in order, so the same code works on a laptop after az login and in Azure with a managed identity.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Running without checking the account or subscription.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-script-that-ran-in-the-wrong-account\">Incident: the script that ran in the wrong account\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An engineer runs a cleanup script that deletes untagged volumes. Their shell still has the production profile exported from the previous day. Forty production volumes are removed before the first alert fires.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Credentials came from ambient shell state, and the script never confirmed where it was running. Add three guards:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import sys\nimport boto3\n\nEXPECTED_ACCOUNT = &quot;111122223333&quot;\naccount = boto3.client(&quot;sts&quot;).get_caller_identity()[&quot;Account&quot;]\nif account != EXPECTED_ACCOUNT:\n    sys.exit(f&quot;refusing to run in account {account}&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> sys\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> boto3\n\nEXPECTED_ACCOUNT = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;111122223333&quot;\u003c/span>\naccount = boto3.client(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sts&quot;\u003c/span>).get_caller_identity()[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Account&quot;\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> account != EXPECTED_ACCOUNT:\n    sys.exit(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;refusing to run in account \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{account}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Pair this with a dry-run default, a tag-based allow list and snapshots before deletion. Production permissions should also be separate roles that require an explicit assume-role step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Hard-coding access keys in scripts or committing them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring pagination and processing only the first page\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running without checking the account or subscription\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing scripts that fail when a resource already exists\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Catching \u003ccode>ClientError\u003c/code> broadly and hiding real permission problems\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"authentication-first","text":"Authentication first"},{"id":"boto3-essentials","text":"boto3 essentials"},{"id":"a-safe-repeatable-task","text":"A safe, repeatable task"},{"id":"the-azure-equivalent","text":"The Azure equivalent"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-script-that-ran-in-the-wrong-account","text":"Incident: the script that ran in the wrong account"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Boto3 documentation","u":"https://boto3.amazonaws.com/v1/documentation/api/latest/index.html"},{"t":"Azure SDK for Python","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/developer/python/"},{"t":"Azure Identity client library for Python","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/python/api/overview/azure/identity-readme"}],"text":"The cloud consoles and CLIs are fine for exploring, but repeatable work belongs in code. Both AWS and Azure publish official Python SDKs, and the patterns you learn for one transfer directly to the other. What you will learn Authenticating with roles and default credential chains Listing and filtering resources with boto3, including pagination The same tasks with the Azure SDK Writing scripts that are safe to run twice Authentication first Never put access keys in a script. On a developer machine use SSO or a profile. On a server or pipeline use an instance role , a workload identity or an assumed role, so the SDK finds short-lived credentials by itself. $ aws sso login --profile dev $ export AWS_PROFILE=dev $ python3 -c \"import boto3; print(boto3.client(&#x27;sts&#x27;).get_caller_identity()[&#x27;Arn&#x27;])\" arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/Developer/reddy Always print the identity first in any script that changes things. Running the right code against the wrong account is a classic way to cause an outage. boto3 essentials import boto3 ec2 = boto3.client( \"ec2\" , region_name= \"eu-west-1\" ) paginator = ec2.get_paginator( \"describe_instances\" ) pages = paginator.paginate( Filters=[{ \"Name\" : \"tag:Env\" , \"Values\" : [ \"prod\" ]}, { \"Name\" : \"instance-state-name\" , \"Values\" : [ \"running\" ]}] ) for page in pages: for reservation in page[ \"Reservations\" ]: for inst in reservation[ \"Instances\" ]: name = next ((t[ \"Value\" ] for t in inst.get( \"Tags\" , []) if t[ \"Key\" ] == \"Name\" ), \"-\" ) print (inst[ \"InstanceId\" ], inst[ \"InstanceType\" ], name) API calls return at most one page of results. A paginator hides the continuation tokens; without it you silently process only the first page. Filter on the server with Filters= instead of downloading everything. A safe, repeatable task Scripts that change infrastructure should be idempotent : running them twice has the same result as once. import boto3 from botocore.exceptions import ClientError s3 = boto3.client( \"s3\" ) def ensure_bucket ( name: str , region: str ) -> None : try : s3.head_bucket(Bucket=name) print ( \"exists:\" , name) except ClientError as exc: if exc.response[ \"Error\" ][ \"Code\" ] not in ( \"404\" , \"NoSuchBucket\" ): raise s3.create_bucket(Bucket=name, CreateBucketConfiguration={ \"LocationConstraint\" : region}) s3.put_public_access_block( Bucket=name, PublicAccessBlockConfiguration={ \"BlockPublicAcls\" : True , \"IgnorePublicAcls\" : True , \"BlockPublicPolicy\" : True , \"RestrictPublicBuckets\" : True }) print ( \"created:\" , name) ensure_bucket( \"acme-reports-eu\" , \"eu-west-1\" ) Check first, create only if missing, and apply the secure settings in the same script. The Azure equivalent from azure.identity import DefaultAzureCredential from azure.mgmt.compute import ComputeManagementClient cred = DefaultAzureCredential() # CLI login, managed identity, or env vars compute = ComputeManagementClient(cred, subscription_id= \"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\" ) for vm in compute.virtual_machines.list_all(): print (vm.name, vm.location, vm.hardware_profile.vm_size) DefaultAzureCredential tries several sources in order, so the same code works on a laptop after az login and in Azure with a managed identity. Install the packages in your virtual environment: $ python -m pip install boto3 azure-identity azure-mgmt-compute $ python -m pip freeze | grep -E \"boto3|azure\" How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Cloud SDKs: boto3 and the Azure SDK fits together Flow in order: 1, Authentication first to boto3 essentials; 2, boto3 essentials to A safe, repeatable task; 3, A safe, repeatable task to The Azure equivalent. Security Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Authentication first Security What it does: Never put access keys in a script. On a developer "},"python/testing-and-packaging":{"html":"\u003cp>A script becomes a tool when it has arguments, tests and a way to install it. These habits turn a one-off helper into something your team trusts at 3 a.m.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Building a command-line interface with \u003ccode>argparse\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing tests with \u003ccode>pytest\u003c/code> and mocking external calls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Packaging with \u003ccode>pyproject.toml\u003c/code> and installing the tool\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running linters and tests in a pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-small-cli\">A small CLI\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import argparse\nimport sys\n\n\ndef parse_args(argv=None):\n    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=&quot;diskwatch&quot;, description=&quot;Warn when disks are nearly full&quot;)\n    p.add_argument(&quot;paths&quot;, nargs=&quot;+&quot;, help=&quot;mount points to check&quot;)\n    p.add_argument(&quot;--threshold&quot;, type=int, default=85, help=&quot;percent used that triggers a warning&quot;)\n    p.add_argument(&quot;--json&quot;, action=&quot;store_true&quot;, help=&quot;print machine-readable output&quot;)\n    return p.parse_args(argv)\n\n\ndef percent_used(path: str) -&gt; int:\n    import shutil\n    usage = shutil.disk_usage(path)\n    return round(usage.used / usage.total * 100)\n\n\ndef main(argv=None) -&gt; int:\n    args = parse_args(argv)\n    bad = [(p, percent_used(p)) for p in args.paths if percent_used(p) &gt;= args.threshold]\n    for path, pct in bad:\n        print(f&quot;WARN {path} is {pct}% full&quot;)\n    return 1 if bad else 0\n\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    sys.exit(main())\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> argparse\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> sys\n\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">parse_args\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">argv=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;diskwatch&quot;\u003c/span>, description=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Warn when disks are nearly full&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    p.add_argument(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;paths&quot;\u003c/span>, nargs=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;+&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">help\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mount points to check&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    p.add_argument(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--threshold&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">int\u003c/span>, default=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">85\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">help\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;percent used that triggers a warning&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    p.add_argument(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--json&quot;\u003c/span>, action=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;store_true&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">help\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;print machine-readable output&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> p.parse_args(argv)\n\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">percent_used\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">path: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">str\u003c/span>\u003c/span>) -&gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">int\u003c/span>:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> shutil\n    usage = shutil.disk_usage(path)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">round\u003c/span>(usage.used / usage.total * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">100\u003c/span>)\n\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">main\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">argv=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>\u003c/span>) -&gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">int\u003c/span>:\n    args = parse_args(argv)\n    bad = [(p, percent_used(p)) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> args.paths \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> percent_used(p) &gt;= args.threshold]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> path, pct \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> bad:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;WARN \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{path}\u003c/span> is \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{pct}\u003c/span>% full&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> bad \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> __name__ == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;__main__&quot;\u003c/span>:\n    sys.exit(main())\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>argparse\u003c/code> generates \u003ccode>--help\u003c/code>, validates types and gives clear errors for free. Returning a non-zero exit code when a check fails lets cron, monitoring and pipelines react.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"testing-with-pytest\">Testing with pytest\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep logic in small functions, then test them. Replace anything slow or dangerous, such as disks or HTTP calls, with a \u003cstrong>mock\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"from unittest import mock\nimport diskwatch\n\n\ndef test_warns_when_over_threshold(capsys):\n    with mock.patch(&quot;diskwatch.percent_used&quot;, return_value=91):\n        code = diskwatch.main([&quot;/data&quot;, &quot;--threshold&quot;, &quot;85&quot;])\n    assert code == 1\n    assert &quot;WARN /data is 91% full&quot; in capsys.readouterr().out\n\n\ndef test_ok_when_under_threshold():\n    with mock.patch(&quot;diskwatch.percent_used&quot;, return_value=40):\n        assert diskwatch.main([&quot;/data&quot;]) == 0\n\n\ndef test_requires_a_path():\n    import pytest\n    with pytest.raises(SystemExit):\n        diskwatch.parse_args([])\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> unittest \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> mock\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> diskwatch\n\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">test_warns_when_over_threshold\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">capsys\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> mock.patch(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;diskwatch.percent_used&quot;\u003c/span>, return_value=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">91\u003c/span>):\n        code = diskwatch.main([\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/data&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--threshold&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;85&quot;\u003c/span>])\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">assert\u003c/span> code == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">assert\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;WARN /data is 91% full&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> capsys.readouterr().out\n\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">test_ok_when_under_threshold\u003c/span>():\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> mock.patch(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;diskwatch.percent_used&quot;\u003c/span>, return_value=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">40\u003c/span>):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">assert\u003c/span> diskwatch.main([\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/data&quot;\u003c/span>]) == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">test_requires_a_path\u003c/span>():\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> pytest\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> pytest.raises(SystemExit):\n        diskwatch.parse_args([])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python -m pip install pytest\npython -m pytest -q\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pip install pytest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pytest -q\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">...                                                                [100%]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">3 passed in 0.04s\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Test the failure paths as hard as the happy path: that is where operations scripts hurt.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"packaging\">Packaging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003ccode>pyproject.toml\u003c/code> describes the project. Installing it creates a real command on the PATH.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[build-system]\nrequires = [&quot;setuptools&gt;=68&quot;]\nbuild-backend = &quot;setuptools.build_meta&quot;\n\n[project]\nname = &quot;diskwatch&quot;\nversion = &quot;0.1.0&quot;\nrequires-python = &quot;&gt;=3.9&quot;\n\n[project.scripts]\ndiskwatch = &quot;diskwatch:main&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">[build-system]\nrequires = [&quot;setuptools&gt;=68&quot;]\nbuild-backend = &quot;setuptools.build_meta&quot;\n\n[project]\nname = &quot;diskwatch&quot;\nversion = &quot;0.1.0&quot;\nrequires-python = &quot;&gt;=3.9&quot;\n\n[project.scripts]\ndiskwatch = &quot;diskwatch:main&quot;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>[project.scripts]\u003c/code> table maps the command name to the function, so \u003ccode>pip install .\u003c/code> creates the \u003ccode>diskwatch\u003c/code> command. Install it into a virtual environment, or with \u003ccode>pipx\u003c/code> so it stays isolated:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python -m pip install .\ndiskwatch /var --threshold 80\ndiskwatch --help\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pip install .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>diskwatch /var --threshold 80\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>diskwatch --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">help\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"checks-in-a-pipeline\">Checks in a pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"test:\n  image: python:3.12-slim\n  script:\n    - python -m pip install -r requirements.txt pytest ruff\n    - ruff check .\n    - python -m pytest -q\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">test:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">python:3.12-slim\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">python\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-m\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pip\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">install\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-r\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">requirements.txt\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pytest\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ruff\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ruff\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">check\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">python\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-m\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pytest\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-q\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Linting catches mistakes before runtime, and the test job blocks a merge when behaviour breaks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Testing and packaging a command-line tool fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A small CLI\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Testing with pytest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Packaging\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Checks in a pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, A small CLI to Testing with pytest; 2, Testing with pytest to Packaging; 3, Packaging to Checks in a pipeline.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>A small CLI \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> argparse generates --help, validates types and gives clear errors for free.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Putting all logic under if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot; where it cannot be tested.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Testing with pytest \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Keep logic in small functions, then test them.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Testing against real servers or production data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Packaging \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A pyproject.toml describes the project. Installing it creates a real command on the PATH.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Pinning nothing, so a new dependency release breaks the build.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Checks in a pipeline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Linting catches mistakes before runtime, and the test job blocks a merge when behaviour breaks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Checks in a pipeline Linting catches mistakes before runtime, and the test job blocks a merge when behaviour breaks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-quiet-change-to-a-shared-script\">Incident: the quiet change to a shared script\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Someone improves a shared \u003ccode>rotate_logs.py\u003c/code> to compress files in place. Nobody tests it. The next night it raises an exception on a file name with spaces, stops halfway, and leaves partial archives. Disk fills up on three hosts by morning.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>With a test suite the problem is found in seconds: add a test with an awkward file name that fails, fix the code, and keep the test forever.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"from pathlib import Path\nimport rotate_logs\n\ndef test_handles_spaces(tmp_path: Path):\n    f = tmp_path / &quot;app log 1.txt&quot;\n    f.write_text(&quot;data&quot;)\n    rotate_logs.compress(f)\n    assert (tmp_path / &quot;app log 1.txt.gz&quot;).exists()\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> pathlib \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> Path\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> rotate_logs\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">test_handles_spaces\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">tmp_path: Path\u003c/span>):\n    f = tmp_path / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app log 1.txt&quot;\u003c/span>\n    f.write_text(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;data&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    rotate_logs.compress(f)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">assert\u003c/span> (tmp_path / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app log 1.txt.gz&quot;\u003c/span>).exists()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>tmp_path\u003c/code> gives each test a clean temporary directory, so tests never touch real data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting all logic under \u003ccode>if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;\u003c/code> where it cannot be tested\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing against real servers or production data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not returning meaningful exit codes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pinning nothing, so a new dependency release breaks the build\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping tests for the error paths\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-small-cli","text":"A small CLI"},{"id":"testing-with-pytest","text":"Testing with pytest"},{"id":"packaging","text":"Packaging"},{"id":"checks-in-a-pipeline","text":"Checks in a pipeline"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-quiet-change-to-a-shared-script","text":"Incident: the quiet change to a shared script"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"pytest documentation","u":"https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/"},{"t":"argparse module","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/argparse.html"},{"t":"Python Packaging User Guide","u":"https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/tutorials/packaging-projects/"}],"text":"A script becomes a tool when it has arguments, tests and a way to install it. These habits turn a one-off helper into something your team trusts at 3 a.m. What you will learn Building a command-line interface with argparse Writing tests with pytest and mocking external calls Packaging with pyproject.toml and installing the tool Running linters and tests in a pipeline A small CLI import argparse import sys def parse_args ( argv= None ): p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog= \"diskwatch\" , description= \"Warn when disks are nearly full\" ) p.add_argument( \"paths\" , nargs= \"+\" , help = \"mount points to check\" ) p.add_argument( \"--threshold\" , type = int , default= 85 , help = \"percent used that triggers a warning\" ) p.add_argument( \"--json\" , action= \"store_true\" , help = \"print machine-readable output\" ) return p.parse_args(argv) def percent_used ( path: str ) -> int : import shutil usage = shutil.disk_usage(path) return round (usage.used / usage.total * 100 ) def main ( argv= None ) -> int : args = parse_args(argv) bad = [(p, percent_used(p)) for p in args.paths if percent_used(p) >= args.threshold] for path, pct in bad: print ( f\"WARN {path} is {pct} % full\" ) return 1 if bad else 0 if __name__ == \"__main__\" : sys.exit(main()) argparse generates --help , validates types and gives clear errors for free. Returning a non-zero exit code when a check fails lets cron, monitoring and pipelines react. Testing with pytest Keep logic in small functions, then test them. Replace anything slow or dangerous, such as disks or HTTP calls, with a mock . from unittest import mock import diskwatch def test_warns_when_over_threshold ( capsys ): with mock.patch( \"diskwatch.percent_used\" , return_value= 91 ): code = diskwatch.main([ \"/data\" , \"--threshold\" , \"85\" ]) assert code == 1 assert \"WARN /data is 91% full\" in capsys.readouterr().out def test_ok_when_under_threshold (): with mock.patch( \"diskwatch.percent_used\" , return_value= 40 ): assert diskwatch.main([ \"/data\" ]) == 0 def test_requires_a_path (): import pytest with pytest.raises(SystemExit): diskwatch.parse_args([]) $ python -m pip install pytest $ python -m pytest -q ... [100%] 3 passed in 0.04s Test the failure paths as hard as the happy path: that is where operations scripts hurt. Packaging A pyproject.toml describes the project. Installing it creates a real command on the PATH. [build-system] requires = [\"setuptools>=68\"] build-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\" [project] name = \"diskwatch\" version = \"0.1.0\" requires-python = \">=3.9\" [project.scripts] diskwatch = \"diskwatch:main\" The [project.scripts] table maps the command name to the function, so pip install . creates the diskwatch command. Install it into a virtual environment, or with pipx so it stays isolated: $ python -m pip install . $ diskwatch /var --threshold 80 $ diskwatch -- help Checks in a pipeline test: image: python:3.12-slim script: - python -m pip install -r requirements.txt pytest ruff - ruff check . - python -m pytest -q Linting catches mistakes before runtime, and the test job blocks a merge when behaviour breaks. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Testing and packaging a command-line tool fits together Flow in order: 1, A small CLI to Testing with pytest; 2, Testing with pytest to Packaging; 3, Packaging to Checks in a pipeline. Client Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. A small CLI Client What it does: argparse generates --help, validates types and gives clear errors for free. If it fails or is weak: Putting all logic under if __name__ == \"__main__\" where it cannot be tested. Testing with pytest Observability What it does: Keep logic in small functions, then test them. If it fails or is weak: Testing against real servers or production data. Packaging Step What it "},"python/concurrency-for-operations":{"html":"\u003cp>Operations scripts spend most of their time waiting: for an API, for a remote host, for a command. Running a hundred checks one after another can take an hour; running them concurrently can take a minute. Python gives you three tools, and choosing the right one and putting limits on it matters more than speed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why waiting work benefits from concurrency and CPU work does not\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Thread pools with \u003ccode>concurrent.futures\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>asyncio\u003c/code> for many network calls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running commands with \u003ccode>subprocess\u003c/code> and timeouts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Limiting concurrency and handling failures\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"which-tool\">Which tool\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Work\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Tool\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Many blocking calls (HTTP, SSH, SDK)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ThreadPoolExecutor\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Very many network connections, async libraries available\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>asyncio\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Heavy CPU work\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ProcessPoolExecutor\u003c/code> or separate processes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>External commands\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>subprocess.run\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>timeout\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The global interpreter lock stops threads from running Python code in parallel, but threads waiting on I/O release it, so thread pools work well for operations tasks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-thread-pool-with-limits\">A thread pool with limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import requests\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, as_completed\n\ndef check(url):\n    r = requests.get(url, timeout=(3, 10))\n    return url, r.status_code\n\nurls = [f&quot;https://svc{i}.example.com/health&quot; for i in range(100)]\nfailed = []\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=20) as pool:\n    futures = {pool.submit(check, u): u for u in urls}\n    for fut in as_completed(futures):\n        try:\n            url, status = fut.result()\n            if status != 200:\n                failed.append((url, status))\n        except Exception as exc:\n            failed.append((futures[fut], repr(exc)))\nprint(f&quot;{len(failed)} problems&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> requests\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> concurrent.futures \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> ThreadPoolExecutor, as_completed\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">check\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">url\u003c/span>):\n    r = requests.get(url, timeout=(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>))\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> url, r.status_code\n\nurls = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;https://svc\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{i}\u003c/span>.example.com/health&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">range\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">100\u003c/span>)]\nfailed = []\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> pool:\n    futures = {pool.submit(check, u): u \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> u \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> urls}\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> fut \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> as_completed(futures):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">try\u003c/span>:\n            url, status = fut.result()\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> status != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">200\u003c/span>:\n                failed.append((url, status))\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> Exception \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> exc:\n            failed.append((futures[fut], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">repr\u003c/span>(exc)))\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(failed)}\u003c/span> problems&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>max_workers\u003c/code> is the limit. Without one, a hundred workers may overload the target or exhaust your file handles. Always set a timeout on each call and collect failures instead of stopping at the first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"asyncio\">asyncio\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import asyncio, aiohttp\n\nasync def check(session, sem, url):\n    async with sem:\n        async with session.get(url, timeout=aiohttp.ClientTimeout(total=10)) as r:\n            return url, r.status\n\nasync def main(urls):\n    sem = asyncio.Semaphore(50)\n    async with aiohttp.ClientSession() as session:\n        return await asyncio.gather(*(check(session, sem, u) for u in urls), return_exceptions=True)\n\nresults = asyncio.run(main(urls))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> asyncio, aiohttp\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">async\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">check\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">session, sem, url\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">async\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> sem:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">async\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> session.get(url, timeout=aiohttp.ClientTimeout(total=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>)) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> r:\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> url, r.status\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">async\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">main\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">urls\u003c/span>):\n    sem = asyncio.Semaphore(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">50\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">async\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> aiohttp.ClientSession() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> session:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">await\u003c/span> asyncio.gather(*(check(session, sem, u) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> u \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> urls), return_exceptions=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>)\n\nresults = asyncio.run(main(urls))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Everything in the call chain must be async, and one blocking call stalls the whole loop. \u003ccode>return_exceptions=True\u003c/code> keeps one failure from cancelling the rest. A \u003ccode>Semaphore\u003c/code> caps concurrent calls.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"running-commands\">Running commands\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import subprocess\np = subprocess.run([&quot;df&quot;, &quot;-h&quot;, &quot;/var&quot;], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10, check=False)\nif p.returncode != 0:\n    raise RuntimeError(p.stderr.strip())\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> subprocess\np = subprocess.run([\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;df&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;-h&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/var&quot;\u003c/span>], capture_output=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>, text=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>, timeout=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>, check=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> p.returncode != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> RuntimeError(p.stderr.strip())\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Pass a list, not a string with \u003ccode>shell=True\u003c/code>, so that input cannot inject commands. Always set \u003ccode>timeout\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You start 500 threads that each call the same API. What is the most likely result?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The API throttles or fails some calls, and your script may run out of connections or memory. A pool with a small max_workers is safer.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Concurrency needs a ceiling. Pair it with timeouts and retries with backoff.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Concurrency for operations: threads, asyncio and... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Which tool\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A thread pool with limits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">asyncio\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Running commands\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Which tool to A thread pool with limits; 2, A thread pool with limits to asyncio; 3, asyncio to Running commands.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Which tool \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The global interpreter lock stops threads from running Python code in parallel, but threads waiting on I/O release it, so thread pools work well for operations tasks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using shell=True with input from outside.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>A thread pool with limits \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> max_workers is the limit. Without one, a hundred workers may overload the target or exhaust your file handles.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stopping the whole run on the first error.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>asyncio \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Everything in the call chain must be async, and one blocking call stalls the whole loop.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Running commands \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pass a list, not a string with shell=True, so that input cannot inject commands.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unlimited concurrency.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-audit-that-took-down-the-inventory-api\">Incident: the audit that took down the inventory API\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new audit script queries every instance&#39;s details at once and the internal inventory API starts returning errors for everyone.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python audit.py\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python audit.py\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">... 3000 requests in 4 seconds\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">429 Too Many Requests\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The script started a thread for each item with no limit. The fix used a pool of 10 workers, a retry with exponential backoff on 429 responses, and a result file written as items finished so a failure did not lose the work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Unlimited concurrency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No timeouts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stopping the whole run on the first error\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>shell=True\u003c/code> with input from outside\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"which-tool","text":"Which tool"},{"id":"a-thread-pool-with-limits","text":"A thread pool with limits"},{"id":"asyncio","text":"asyncio"},{"id":"running-commands","text":"Running commands"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-audit-that-took-down-the-inventory-api","text":"Incident: the audit that took down the inventory API"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Python docs: concurrent.futures","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/concurrent.futures.html"},{"t":"Python docs: asyncio","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio.html"}],"text":"Operations scripts spend most of their time waiting: for an API, for a remote host, for a command. Running a hundred checks one after another can take an hour; running them concurrently can take a minute. Python gives you three tools, and choosing the right one and putting limits on it matters more than speed. What you will learn Why waiting work benefits from concurrency and CPU work does not Thread pools with concurrent.futures asyncio for many network calls Running commands with subprocess and timeouts Limiting concurrency and handling failures Which tool Work Tool Many blocking calls (HTTP, SSH, SDK) ThreadPoolExecutor Very many network connections, async libraries available asyncio Heavy CPU work ProcessPoolExecutor or separate processes External commands subprocess.run with timeout The global interpreter lock stops threads from running Python code in parallel, but threads waiting on I/O release it, so thread pools work well for operations tasks. A thread pool with limits import requests from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, as_completed def check ( url ): r = requests.get(url, timeout=( 3 , 10 )) return url, r.status_code urls = [ f\"https://svc {i} .example.com/health\" for i in range ( 100 )] failed = [] with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers= 20 ) as pool: futures = {pool.submit(check, u): u for u in urls} for fut in as_completed(futures): try : url, status = fut.result() if status != 200 : failed.append((url, status)) except Exception as exc: failed.append((futures[fut], repr (exc))) print ( f\" { len (failed)} problems\" ) max_workers is the limit. Without one, a hundred workers may overload the target or exhaust your file handles. Always set a timeout on each call and collect failures instead of stopping at the first. asyncio import asyncio, aiohttp async def check ( session, sem, url ): async with sem: async with session.get(url, timeout=aiohttp.ClientTimeout(total= 10 )) as r: return url, r.status async def main ( urls ): sem = asyncio.Semaphore( 50 ) async with aiohttp.ClientSession() as session: return await asyncio.gather(*(check(session, sem, u) for u in urls), return_exceptions= True ) results = asyncio.run(main(urls)) Everything in the call chain must be async, and one blocking call stalls the whole loop. return_exceptions=True keeps one failure from cancelling the rest. A Semaphore caps concurrent calls. Running commands import subprocess p = subprocess.run([ \"df\" , \"-h\" , \"/var\" ], capture_output= True , text= True , timeout= 10 , check= False ) if p.returncode != 0 : raise RuntimeError(p.stderr.strip()) Pass a list, not a string with shell=True , so that input cannot inject commands. Always set timeout . Predict first. You start 500 threads that each call the same API. What is the most likely result? Your guess Answer: The API throttles or fails some calls, and your script may run out of connections or memory. A pool with a small max_workers is safer. Concurrency needs a ceiling. Pair it with timeouts and retries with backoff. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Concurrency for operations: threads, asyncio and... fits together Flow in order: 1, Which tool to A thread pool with limits; 2, A thread pool with limits to asyncio; 3, asyncio to Running commands. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Which tool Step What it does: The global interpreter lock stops threads from running Python code in parallel, but threads waiting on I/O release it, so thread pools work well for operations tasks. If it fails or is weak: Using shell=True with input from outside. A thread pool with limits Step What it does: max_workers is the limit. Without one, a hundred workers may overload the target or exhaust your file handles. If it fails or is weak: Stopping the whole run on the fi"},"python/writing-command-line-tools":{"html":"\u003cp>Many operations scripts start as twenty lines and grow into tools that a whole team runs. A good command-line tool is predictable: it has help text, validates input, logs sensibly, returns the right exit code and can be tested. Python&#39;s standard library gives you everything you need to get there.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Defining arguments and subcommands with \u003ccode>argparse\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading configuration from options, environment variables and files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Human and machine-readable output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging, exit codes and error handling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing a command-line tool and packaging an entry point\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"arguments-with-argparse\">Arguments with argparse\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import argparse, logging, sys\n\ndef build_parser():\n    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=&quot;svcctl&quot;, description=&quot;Inspect services&quot;)\n    p.add_argument(&quot;-v&quot;, &quot;--verbose&quot;, action=&quot;store_true&quot;, help=&quot;show debug logs&quot;)\n    sub = p.add_subparsers(dest=&quot;cmd&quot;, required=True)\n    s = sub.add_parser(&quot;status&quot;, help=&quot;show service status&quot;)\n    s.add_argument(&quot;name&quot;)\n    s.add_argument(&quot;--json&quot;, action=&quot;store_true&quot;, help=&quot;machine-readable output&quot;)\n    s.add_argument(&quot;--timeout&quot;, type=float, default=5.0)\n    return p\n\ndef main(argv=None):\n    args = build_parser().parse_args(argv)\n    logging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG if args.verbose else logging.WARNING,\n                        format=&quot;%(levelname)s %(message)s&quot;, stream=sys.stderr)\n    try:\n        return run(args)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        return 130\n    except Exception as exc:\n        logging.error(&quot;%s&quot;, exc)\n        return 1\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    sys.exit(main())\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> argparse, logging, sys\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">build_parser\u003c/span>():\n    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;svcctl&quot;\u003c/span>, description=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Inspect services&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    p.add_argument(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;-v&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--verbose&quot;\u003c/span>, action=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;store_true&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">help\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;show debug logs&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    sub = p.add_subparsers(dest=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cmd&quot;\u003c/span>, required=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>)\n    s = sub.add_parser(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">help\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;show service status&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    s.add_argument(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    s.add_argument(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--json&quot;\u003c/span>, action=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;store_true&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">help\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;machine-readable output&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    s.add_argument(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--timeout&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">float\u003c/span>, default=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5.0\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> p\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">main\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">argv=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    args = build_parser().parse_args(argv)\n    logging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> args.verbose \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> logging.WARNING,\n                        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">format\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;%(levelname)s %(message)s&quot;\u003c/span>, stream=sys.stderr)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">try\u003c/span>:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> run(args)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> KeyboardInterrupt:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">130\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> Exception \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> exc:\n        logging.error(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;%s&quot;\u003c/span>, exc)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> __name__ == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;__main__&quot;\u003c/span>:\n    sys.exit(main())\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>argparse\u003c/code> builds \u003ccode>--help\u003c/code>, checks types and rejects unknown options with exit code 2. Taking \u003ccode>argv\u003c/code> as a parameter makes \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> easy to test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"configuration-order\">Configuration order\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use this order of precedence: command-line option, then environment variable, then a config file, then a default. Document it. Read secrets from the environment or a secret store, never from a flag (flags show up in process lists and shell history).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import os\ntoken = os.environ.get(&quot;SVC_TOKEN&quot;)\nif not token:\n    raise SystemExit(&quot;SVC_TOKEN is not set&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> os\ntoken = os.environ.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SVC_TOKEN&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> token:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> SystemExit(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SVC_TOKEN is not set&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"output\">Output\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Print results to standard output and logs and errors to standard error. Offer \u003ccode>--json\u003c/code> for scripts and a readable table for people. Never mix log lines into JSON output. Keep field names stable, because other tools will depend on them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exit-codes\">Exit codes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>0 means success, 1 a general failure, 2 a usage error (the \u003ccode>argparse\u003c/code> default) and 130 an interrupt. If your tool is used in checks, define extra codes (for example 3 for &quot;degraded&quot;) and document them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"testing\">Testing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def test_status_json(capsys, monkeypatch):\n    monkeypatch.setattr(&quot;svcctl.fetch&quot;, lambda name, timeout: {&quot;name&quot;: name, &quot;ok&quot;: True})\n    code = main([&quot;status&quot;, &quot;web&quot;, &quot;--json&quot;])\n    out = capsys.readouterr().out\n    assert code == 0 and '&quot;ok&quot;: true' in out\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">test_status_json\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">capsys, monkeypatch\u003c/span>):\n    monkeypatch.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">setattr\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;svcctl.fetch&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda\u003c/span> name, timeout: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: name, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ok&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>})\n    code = main([\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--json&quot;\u003c/span>])\n    out = capsys.readouterr().out\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">assert\u003c/span> code == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;&quot;ok&quot;: true&#x27;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Test the function, not the process. Use \u003ccode>subprocess\u003c/code> only for a small number of end-to-end checks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"packaging\">Packaging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In \u003ccode>pyproject.toml\u003c/code>, add \u003ccode>[project.scripts]\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>svcctl = &quot;svcctl.cli:main&quot;\u003c/code>. Installing the package (preferably with \u003ccode>pipx\u003c/code>) creates a \u003ccode>svcctl\u003c/code> command. Pin dependencies in a lock file and include a version flag (\u003ccode>--version\u003c/code>).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A user runs your tool as `svcctl status` with no name. What should happen?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> argparse prints a usage error to standard error and exits with code 2.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Required arguments are validated before your code runs, so no custom handling is needed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Writing command-line tools in Python fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Arguments with argparse\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Configuration order\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Output\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Exit codes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Testing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Packaging\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Arguments with argparse to Configuration order; 2, Configuration order to Output; 3, Output to Exit codes; 4, Exit codes to Testing; 5, Testing to Packaging.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Arguments with argparse \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> argparse builds --help, checks types and rejects unknown options with exit code 2.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Printing logs on standard output.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Configuration order \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Use this order of precedence: command-line option, then environment variable, then a config file, then a default.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Secrets passed as flags.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Output \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Print results to standard output and logs and errors to standard error.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never mix log lines into JSON output.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Exit codes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> 0 means success, 1 a general failure, 2 a usage error (the argparse default) and 130 an interrupt.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unclear exit codes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Testing \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Test the function, not the process. Use subprocess only for a small number of end-to-end checks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Code in if __name__ blocks that cannot be tested.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>Packaging \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> In pyproject.toml, add [project.scripts] with svcctl = &quot;svcctl.cli:main&quot;.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-json-that-wasn-t-json\">Incident: the JSON that wasn&#39;t JSON\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A monitoring job that parses the tool&#39;s \u003ccode>--json\u003c/code> output starts failing after a release.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"svcctl status web --json | head -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>svcctl status web --json | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">INFO connecting to web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;name&quot;: &quot;web&quot;, &quot;ok&quot;: true}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A new log line went to standard output and broke the parser. The fix sent logs to standard error, added a test that parses the output as JSON, and documented that standard output contains only the result.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Printing logs on standard output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets passed as flags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unclear exit codes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Code in \u003ccode>if __name__\u003c/code> blocks that cannot be tested\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"arguments-with-argparse","text":"Arguments with argparse"},{"id":"configuration-order","text":"Configuration order"},{"id":"output","text":"Output"},{"id":"exit-codes","text":"Exit codes"},{"id":"testing","text":"Testing"},{"id":"packaging","text":"Packaging"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-json-that-wasn-t-json","text":"Incident: the JSON that wasn't JSON"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Python docs: argparse","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/argparse.html"},{"t":"Python Packaging User Guide","u":"https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/"}],"text":"Many operations scripts start as twenty lines and grow into tools that a whole team runs. A good command-line tool is predictable: it has help text, validates input, logs sensibly, returns the right exit code and can be tested. Python&#39;s standard library gives you everything you need to get there. What you will learn Defining arguments and subcommands with argparse Reading configuration from options, environment variables and files Human and machine-readable output Logging, exit codes and error handling Testing a command-line tool and packaging an entry point Arguments with argparse import argparse, logging, sys def build_parser (): p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog= \"svcctl\" , description= \"Inspect services\" ) p.add_argument( \"-v\" , \"--verbose\" , action= \"store_true\" , help = \"show debug logs\" ) sub = p.add_subparsers(dest= \"cmd\" , required= True ) s = sub.add_parser( \"status\" , help = \"show service status\" ) s.add_argument( \"name\" ) s.add_argument( \"--json\" , action= \"store_true\" , help = \"machine-readable output\" ) s.add_argument( \"--timeout\" , type = float , default= 5.0 ) return p def main ( argv= None ): args = build_parser().parse_args(argv) logging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG if args.verbose else logging.WARNING, format = \"%(levelname)s %(message)s\" , stream=sys.stderr) try : return run(args) except KeyboardInterrupt: return 130 except Exception as exc: logging.error( \"%s\" , exc) return 1 if __name__ == \"__main__\" : sys.exit(main()) argparse builds --help , checks types and rejects unknown options with exit code 2. Taking argv as a parameter makes main easy to test. Configuration order Use this order of precedence: command-line option, then environment variable, then a config file, then a default. Document it. Read secrets from the environment or a secret store, never from a flag (flags show up in process lists and shell history). import os token = os.environ.get( \"SVC_TOKEN\" ) if not token: raise SystemExit( \"SVC_TOKEN is not set\" ) Output Print results to standard output and logs and errors to standard error. Offer --json for scripts and a readable table for people. Never mix log lines into JSON output. Keep field names stable, because other tools will depend on them. Exit codes 0 means success, 1 a general failure, 2 a usage error (the argparse default) and 130 an interrupt. If your tool is used in checks, define extra codes (for example 3 for \"degraded\") and document them. Testing def test_status_json ( capsys, monkeypatch ): monkeypatch. setattr ( \"svcctl.fetch\" , lambda name, timeout: { \"name\" : name, \"ok\" : True }) code = main([ \"status\" , \"web\" , \"--json\" ]) out = capsys.readouterr().out assert code == 0 and &#x27;\"ok\": true&#x27; in out Test the function, not the process. Use subprocess only for a small number of end-to-end checks. Packaging In pyproject.toml , add [project.scripts] with svcctl = \"svcctl.cli:main\" . Installing the package (preferably with pipx ) creates a svcctl command. Pin dependencies in a lock file and include a version flag ( --version ). Predict first. A user runs your tool as `svcctl status` with no name. What should happen? Your guess Answer: argparse prints a usage error to standard error and exits with code 2. Required arguments are validated before your code runs, so no custom handling is needed. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Writing command-line tools in Python fits together Flow in order: 1, Arguments with argparse to Configuration order; 2, Configuration order to Output; 3, Output to Exit codes; 4, Exit codes to Testing; 5, Testing to Packaging. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Arguments with argparse Step What it does: argparse builds --help, checks types and rejects unknown options with exit code 2. If it fai"},"python/logging-for-scripts-and-services":{"html":"\u003cp>A nightly script prints &quot;done&quot; and nothing else, so when it breaks nobody knows which step failed or how long it took. Using \u003ccode>print\u003c/code> for everything also mixes results with diagnostics, so you cannot send one to a file and the other to the screen. Python ships a logging module that solves this in a few lines.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Levels: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Configure format, destination and level once at start-up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Log exceptions with tracebacks and useful context\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Structured (JSON) logs that tools can search\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"basic-setup\">Basic setup\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Configure logging once, in the main program, not in libraries. Every module then asks for a logger by name.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(\n    level=logging.INFO,\n    format=&quot;%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(name)s: %(message)s&quot;,\n)\nlog = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nlog.info(&quot;starting export for %s&quot;, &quot;2026-01-05&quot;)\nlog.warning(&quot;retrying page %d&quot;, 7)\nlog.error(&quot;export failed&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(\n    level=logging.INFO,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">format\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(name)s: %(message)s&quot;\u003c/span>,\n)\nlog = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nlog.info(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;starting export for %s&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2026-01-05&quot;\u003c/span>)\nlog.warning(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;retrying page %d&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7\u003c/span>)\nlog.error(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;export failed&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>%s\u003c/code> placeholders and pass values as arguments instead of building the string with an f-string. The text is only built if the message is actually emitted, which saves time on DEBUG lines in a tight loop.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choose-levels-on-purpose\">Choose levels on purpose\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Use it for\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>DEBUG\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Details you need only when diagnosing a problem\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>INFO\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Normal milestones: started, finished, counts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>WARNING\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Something unexpected, but the work continues\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>ERROR\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An operation failed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>CRITICAL\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The program cannot continue\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Production usually runs at INFO. Make the level a setting (for example the \u003ccode>LOG_LEVEL\u003c/code> environment variable) so that you can raise it to DEBUG for one run without changing code. Avoid logging at ERROR for things that are normal, because people stop trusting the signal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"log-failures-with-context\">Log failures with context\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>log.exception\u003c/code> records an error message together with the full traceback, and is meant to be used inside an \u003ccode>except\u003c/code> block.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"try:\n    push_report(day)\nexcept requests.Timeout:\n    log.exception(&quot;report push timed out day=%s&quot;, day)\n    raise\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">try\u003c/span>:\n    push_report(day)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> requests.Timeout:\n    log.exception(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;report push timed out day=%s&quot;\u003c/span>, day)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Include the identifiers someone needs to find the case: the file name, the customer id, the request id, the attempt number. Never log secrets, tokens or full card numbers. A good error line answers three questions: what failed, for which item, and what happens next.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"files-rotation-and-json\">Files, rotation and JSON\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scripts that run under systemd or in a container should write to standard output and let the platform collect it. For a plain server, use a rotating file handler so the log cannot fill the disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"from logging.handlers import RotatingFileHandler\nhandler = RotatingFileHandler(&quot;/var/log/report.log&quot;, maxBytes=5_000_000, backupCount=5)\nlogging.getLogger().addHandler(handler)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> logging.handlers \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> RotatingFileHandler\nhandler = RotatingFileHandler(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/var/log/report.log&quot;\u003c/span>, maxBytes=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5_000_000\u003c/span>, backupCount=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>)\nlogging.getLogger().addHandler(handler)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For log search tools, one JSON object per line is easier to query than free text. A small custom formatter or a library can produce lines such as \u003ccode>{&quot;ts&quot;: &quot;...&quot;, &quot;level&quot;: &quot;ERROR&quot;, &quot;msg&quot;: &quot;push failed&quot;, &quot;day&quot;: &quot;2026-01-05&quot;}\u003c/code>. Keep the field names the same in every program.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Why is `log.info(&quot;user %s&quot;, name)` better than `log.info(f&quot;user {name}&quot;)` in a hot loop?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The first builds the message only when INFO is enabled, and lets handlers see the raw template and arguments.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Lazy formatting avoids wasted work and keeps the template available for grouping similar messages.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Logging for scripts and services fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Basic setup\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Choose levels on purpose\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Log failures with context\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Files, rotation and JSON\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Basic setup to Choose levels on purpose; 2, Choose levels on purpose to Log failures with context; 3, Log failures with context to Files, rotation and JSON.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Basic setup \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Configure logging once, in the main program, not in libraries.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Calling basicConfig inside a library, which fights the application settings.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Choose levels on purpose \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Production usually runs at INFO. Make the level a setting (for example the LOG_LEVEL environment variable) so that you can raise it to DEBUG for one run without changing...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Catching an exception and logging nothing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Log failures with context \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> log.exception records an error message together with the full traceback, and is meant to be used inside an except block.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Logging secrets or personal data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Files, rotation and JSON \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Scripts that run under systemd or in a container should write to standard output and let the platform collect it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using print for diagnostics and results at the same time.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-done-but-did-it-work\">Incident: Done, but did it work?\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly sync ended with the line &quot;done&quot;, yet customers were missing from the next morning report.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"tail -3 /var/log/sync.out\ngrep -c ERROR /var/log/sync.out\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3 /var/log/sync.out\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">fetching page 41\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">fetching page 42\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">done\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c ERROR /var/log/sync.out\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The script caught every exception and printed nothing, so a timeout on page 38 was lost. After the change it logged a warning for each retry, an error with the page number when a page was skipped, and a final INFO line with counts (\u003ccode>fetched=412 skipped=1\u003c/code>). The scheduler marked the run as failed whenever skipped was above zero, so the next gap was reported within minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using print for diagnostics and results at the same time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Catching an exception and logging nothing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging secrets or personal data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Calling basicConfig inside a library, which fights the application settings\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"basic-setup","text":"Basic setup"},{"id":"choose-levels-on-purpose","text":"Choose levels on purpose"},{"id":"log-failures-with-context","text":"Log failures with context"},{"id":"files-rotation-and-json","text":"Files, rotation and JSON"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-done-but-did-it-work","text":"Incident: Done, but did it work?"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Python logging HOWTO","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/howto/logging.html"},{"t":"Python logging cookbook","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/howto/logging-cookbook.html"}],"text":"A nightly script prints \"done\" and nothing else, so when it breaks nobody knows which step failed or how long it took. Using print for everything also mixes results with diagnostics, so you cannot send one to a file and the other to the screen. Python ships a logging module that solves this in a few lines. What you will learn Levels: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL Configure format, destination and level once at start-up Log exceptions with tracebacks and useful context Structured (JSON) logs that tools can search Basic setup Configure logging once, in the main program, not in libraries. Every module then asks for a logger by name. import logging logging.basicConfig( level=logging.INFO, format = \"%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(name)s: %(message)s\" , ) log = logging.getLogger(__name__) log.info( \"starting export for %s\" , \"2026-01-05\" ) log.warning( \"retrying page %d\" , 7 ) log.error( \"export failed\" ) Use %s placeholders and pass values as arguments instead of building the string with an f-string. The text is only built if the message is actually emitted, which saves time on DEBUG lines in a tight loop. Choose levels on purpose Level Use it for DEBUG Details you need only when diagnosing a problem INFO Normal milestones: started, finished, counts WARNING Something unexpected, but the work continues ERROR An operation failed CRITICAL The program cannot continue Production usually runs at INFO. Make the level a setting (for example the LOG_LEVEL environment variable) so that you can raise it to DEBUG for one run without changing code. Avoid logging at ERROR for things that are normal, because people stop trusting the signal. Log failures with context log.exception records an error message together with the full traceback, and is meant to be used inside an except block. try : push_report(day) except requests.Timeout: log.exception( \"report push timed out day=%s\" , day) raise Include the identifiers someone needs to find the case: the file name, the customer id, the request id, the attempt number. Never log secrets, tokens or full card numbers. A good error line answers three questions: what failed, for which item, and what happens next. Files, rotation and JSON Scripts that run under systemd or in a container should write to standard output and let the platform collect it. For a plain server, use a rotating file handler so the log cannot fill the disk. from logging.handlers import RotatingFileHandler handler = RotatingFileHandler( \"/var/log/report.log\" , maxBytes= 5_000_000 , backupCount= 5 ) logging.getLogger().addHandler(handler) For log search tools, one JSON object per line is easier to query than free text. A small custom formatter or a library can produce lines such as {\"ts\": \"...\", \"level\": \"ERROR\", \"msg\": \"push failed\", \"day\": \"2026-01-05\"} . Keep the field names the same in every program. Predict first. Why is `log.info(\"user %s\", name)` better than `log.info(f\"user {name}\")` in a hot loop? Your guess Answer: The first builds the message only when INFO is enabled, and lets handlers see the raw template and arguments. Lazy formatting avoids wasted work and keeps the template available for grouping similar messages. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Logging for scripts and services fits together Flow in order: 1, Basic setup to Choose levels on purpose; 2, Choose levels on purpose to Log failures with context; 3, Log failures with context to Files, rotation and JSON. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Basic setup Step What it does: Configure logging once, in the main program, not in libraries. If it fails or is weak: Calling basicConfig inside a library, which fights the application settings. Choose levels on purpose Step What it does: Production usually runs at "},"python/yaml-csv-and-config-files":{"html":"\u003cp>Infrastructure is described in files. Kubernetes manifests are YAML, exports from a spreadsheet are CSV, and older tools use INI. Python can read them all, but each has traps: YAML turns the word \u003ccode>no\u003c/code> into a boolean, CSV breaks on commas inside fields, and loading untrusted YAML the wrong way can run code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Parse YAML with safe_load and understand type surprises\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use the csv module instead of splitting on commas\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read INI-style files with configparser\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Validate what you read before you use it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"yaml-always-safe-load\">YAML: always safe_load\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>YAML is not in the standard library; install PyYAML. Always use \u003ccode>safe_load\u003c/code>, which only builds plain data. The plain \u003ccode>load\u003c/code> function can create arbitrary Python objects from a crafted file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import yaml\n\nwith open(&quot;deploy.yaml&quot;) as f:\n    data = yaml.safe_load(f)\n\nreplicas = data[&quot;spec&quot;][&quot;replicas&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> yaml\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">open\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deploy.yaml&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> f:\n    data = yaml.safe_load(f)\n\nreplicas = data[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;spec&quot;\u003c/span>][\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;replicas&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Watch the type rules. Unquoted \u003ccode>no\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>off\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>yes\u003c/code> become booleans in YAML 1.1, so a country code \u003ccode>NO\u003c/code> can turn into \u003ccode>False\u003c/code>. A version such as \u003ccode>1.10\u003c/code> becomes the number 1.1. Quote strings that must stay text, and write files with \u003ccode>yaml.safe_dump(data, sort_keys=False)\u003c/code> to keep key order.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"csv-use-the-module\">CSV: use the module\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Splitting a line on commas breaks as soon as a value contains a comma or a quote. The \u003ccode>csv\u003c/code> module handles quoting for you.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import csv\n\nwith open(&quot;servers.csv&quot;, newline=&quot;&quot;) as f:\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        print(row[&quot;name&quot;], row[&quot;cpu&quot;])\n\nwith open(&quot;out.csv&quot;, &quot;w&quot;, newline=&quot;&quot;) as f:\n    w = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=[&quot;name&quot;, &quot;cpu&quot;])\n    w.writeheader()\n    w.writerow({&quot;name&quot;: &quot;web, east&quot;, &quot;cpu&quot;: 4})\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> csv\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">open\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;servers.csv&quot;\u003c/span>, newline=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> f:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> row \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> csv.DictReader(f):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(row[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>], row[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cpu&quot;\u003c/span>])\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">open\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;out.csv&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;w&quot;\u003c/span>, newline=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> f:\n    w = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cpu&quot;\u003c/span>])\n    w.writeheader()\n    w.writerow({\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web, east&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cpu&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>})\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Open CSV files with \u003ccode>newline=&quot;&quot;\u003c/code>, as the documentation says, and convert numbers yourself, because every value is text. For big files, loop over rows instead of loading all of them into a list.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ini-files-with-configparser\">INI files with configparser\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Many tools still use \u003ccode>[section]\u003c/code> files with \u003ccode>key = value\u003c/code> lines. \u003ccode>configparser\u003c/code> reads them and supports defaults and typed getters.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import configparser\n\ncfg = configparser.ConfigParser()\ncfg.read(&quot;app.ini&quot;)\nport = cfg.getint(&quot;server&quot;, &quot;port&quot;, fallback=8080)\ndebug = cfg.getboolean(&quot;server&quot;, &quot;debug&quot;, fallback=False)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> configparser\n\ncfg = configparser.ConfigParser()\ncfg.read(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app.ini&quot;\u003c/span>)\nport = cfg.getint(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;server&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;port&quot;\u003c/span>, fallback=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>)\ndebug = cfg.getboolean(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;server&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;debug&quot;\u003c/span>, fallback=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Keys are case-insensitive by default, and \u003ccode>%\u003c/code> has special meaning in values unless you use the raw parser.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validate-before-you-trust\">Validate before you trust\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A file that parses is not necessarily valid. Check required keys, types and ranges at the point where you read, and fail with a message that names the file and the key.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def require(data, key, kind):\n    if key not in data:\n        raise ValueError(f&quot;deploy.yaml: missing key {key!r}&quot;)\n    if not isinstance(data[key], kind):\n        raise ValueError(f&quot;deploy.yaml: {key!r} must be {kind.__name__}&quot;)\n    return data[key]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">require\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">data, key, kind\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> key \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> data:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> ValueError(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;deploy.yaml: missing key \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{key!r}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">isinstance\u003c/span>(data[key], kind):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> ValueError(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;deploy.yaml: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{key!r}\u003c/span> must be \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{kind.__name__}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> data[key]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For larger schemas, a library such as \u003ccode>jsonschema\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>pydantic\u003c/code> describes the rules in one place and reports every error at once.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A YAML file has `country: NO`. What does `yaml.safe_load` return for that value, and how do you fix the file?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> It returns the boolean False in YAML 1.1 parsers such as PyYAML. Quote it: `country: &quot;NO&quot;`.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Unquoted yes, no, on and off are booleans in YAML 1.1.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Working with YAML, CSV and configuration files fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">YAML: always safe_load\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CSV: use the module\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">INI files with configparser\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Validate before you trust\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, YAML: always safe_load to CSV: use the module; 2, CSV: use the module to INI files with configparser; 3, INI files with configparser to Validate before you trust.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>YAML: always safe_load \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> YAML is not in the standard library; install PyYAML.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using yaml.load on files you did not write.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>CSV: use the module \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Splitting a line on commas breaks as soon as a value contains a comma or a quote.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Splitting CSV lines with split(&quot;,&quot;).\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>INI files with configparser \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Many tools still use [section] files with key = value lines.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgetting newline=&quot;&quot; when opening CSV files.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Validate before you trust \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A file that parses is not necessarily valid.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Trusting parsed data without checking required keys.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-list-that-lost-a-server\">Incident: The list that lost a server\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A script that read an inventory exported from a spreadsheet silently dropped one server from a maintenance run.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -n &quot;db,east&quot; inventory.csv\npython3 -c &quot;print('db,east,4,16'.split(','))&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db,east&quot;\u003c/span> inventory.csv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">17:db,east,4,16\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python3 -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;print(&#x27;db,east,4,16&#x27;.split(&#x27;,&#x27;))&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">['db', 'east', '4', '16']\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The exporter wrote the name as \u003ccode>&quot;db, east&quot;\u003c/code> with quotes, and the script split lines on commas, so the columns shifted and the host failed a numeric check and was skipped. Switching to \u003ccode>csv.DictReader\u003c/code> fixed it. The team also added a check that compares the number of rows read with the number of lines in the file and stops on a mismatch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using yaml.load on files you did not write\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Splitting CSV lines with split(&quot;,&quot;)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting newline=&quot;&quot; when opening CSV files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting parsed data without checking required keys\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"yaml-always-safe-load","text":"YAML: always safe_load"},{"id":"csv-use-the-module","text":"CSV: use the module"},{"id":"ini-files-with-configparser","text":"INI files with configparser"},{"id":"validate-before-you-trust","text":"Validate before you trust"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-list-that-lost-a-server","text":"Incident: The list that lost a server"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"csv module documentation","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/csv.html"},{"t":"configparser documentation","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/configparser.html"}],"text":"Infrastructure is described in files. Kubernetes manifests are YAML, exports from a spreadsheet are CSV, and older tools use INI. Python can read them all, but each has traps: YAML turns the word no into a boolean, CSV breaks on commas inside fields, and loading untrusted YAML the wrong way can run code. What you will learn Parse YAML with safe_load and understand type surprises Use the csv module instead of splitting on commas Read INI-style files with configparser Validate what you read before you use it YAML: always safe_load YAML is not in the standard library; install PyYAML. Always use safe_load , which only builds plain data. The plain load function can create arbitrary Python objects from a crafted file. import yaml with open ( \"deploy.yaml\" ) as f: data = yaml.safe_load(f) replicas = data[ \"spec\" ][ \"replicas\" ] Watch the type rules. Unquoted no , off and yes become booleans in YAML 1.1, so a country code NO can turn into False . A version such as 1.10 becomes the number 1.1. Quote strings that must stay text, and write files with yaml.safe_dump(data, sort_keys=False) to keep key order. CSV: use the module Splitting a line on commas breaks as soon as a value contains a comma or a quote. The csv module handles quoting for you. import csv with open ( \"servers.csv\" , newline= \"\" ) as f: for row in csv.DictReader(f): print (row[ \"name\" ], row[ \"cpu\" ]) with open ( \"out.csv\" , \"w\" , newline= \"\" ) as f: w = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=[ \"name\" , \"cpu\" ]) w.writeheader() w.writerow({ \"name\" : \"web, east\" , \"cpu\" : 4 }) Open CSV files with newline=\"\" , as the documentation says, and convert numbers yourself, because every value is text. For big files, loop over rows instead of loading all of them into a list. INI files with configparser Many tools still use [section] files with key = value lines. configparser reads them and supports defaults and typed getters. import configparser cfg = configparser.ConfigParser() cfg.read( \"app.ini\" ) port = cfg.getint( \"server\" , \"port\" , fallback= 8080 ) debug = cfg.getboolean( \"server\" , \"debug\" , fallback= False ) Keys are case-insensitive by default, and % has special meaning in values unless you use the raw parser. Validate before you trust A file that parses is not necessarily valid. Check required keys, types and ranges at the point where you read, and fail with a message that names the file and the key. def require ( data, key, kind ): if key not in data: raise ValueError( f\"deploy.yaml: missing key {key!r} \" ) if not isinstance (data[key], kind): raise ValueError( f\"deploy.yaml: {key!r} must be {kind.__name__} \" ) return data[key] For larger schemas, a library such as jsonschema or pydantic describes the rules in one place and reports every error at once. Predict first. A YAML file has `country: NO`. What does `yaml.safe_load` return for that value, and how do you fix the file? Your guess Answer: It returns the boolean False in YAML 1.1 parsers such as PyYAML. Quote it: `country: \"NO\"`. Unquoted yes, no, on and off are booleans in YAML 1.1. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Working with YAML, CSV and configuration files fits together Flow in order: 1, YAML: always safe_load to CSV: use the module; 2, CSV: use the module to INI files with configparser; 3, INI files with configparser to Validate before you trust. Network edge Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. YAML: always safe_load Network edge What it does: YAML is not in the standard library; install PyYAML. If it fails or is weak: Using yaml.load on files you did not write. CSV: use the module Step What it does: Splitting a line on commas breaks as soon as a value contains a comma or a quote. If it fails or is weak: Splitting CSV lines with split(\",\"). INI files with configparser Step W"},"python/debugging-python":{"html":"\u003cp>Python usually tells you what went wrong, and in which line. Many hours are lost because people read the top of the error instead of the bottom, add print statements everywhere, or cannot see why a script simply hangs. This lesson shows a calm routine for finding the cause.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading a traceback correctly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>logging\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>print\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stepping through code with \u003ccode>breakpoint()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pdb\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Finding a script that hangs, with \u003ccode>faulthandler\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>py-spy\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reproducing a failure with the smallest possible input\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-the-traceback-from-the-bottom\">Read the traceback from the bottom\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python sync.py\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python sync.py\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Traceback (most recent call last):\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  File &quot;sync.py&quot;, line 31, in &lt;module&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    main()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  File &quot;sync.py&quot;, line 24, in main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    total += item[&quot;price&quot;] * item[&quot;qty&quot;]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">KeyError: 'qty'\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The last line names the error and the value (\u003ccode>KeyError: &#39;qty&#39;\u003c/code>). The line above it shows where it happened. The frames above that show how the code got there. Here an item has no \u003ccode>qty\u003c/code>. Decide whether the data is wrong or the code should cope (\u003ccode>item.get(&quot;qty&quot;, 0)\u003c/code>), and then add a test with that input.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"logging-beats-print\">Logging beats print\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import logging\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=&quot;%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(name)s %(message)s&quot;)\nlog = logging.getLogger(&quot;sync&quot;)\n\nlog.info(&quot;fetching %s&quot;, url)\ntry:\n    resp = session.get(url, timeout=10)\n    resp.raise_for_status()\nexcept Exception:\n    log.exception(&quot;fetch failed for %s&quot;, url)   # includes the traceback\n    raise\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> logging\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">format\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(name)s %(message)s&quot;\u003c/span>)\nlog = logging.getLogger(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sync&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\nlog.info(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;fetching %s&quot;\u003c/span>, url)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">try\u003c/span>:\n    resp = session.get(url, timeout=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>)\n    resp.raise_for_status()\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> Exception:\n    log.exception(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;fetch failed for %s&quot;\u003c/span>, url)   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># includes the traceback\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Levels let you keep debug detail switched off in normal runs. \u003ccode>log.exception\u003c/code> records the full traceback. Log identifiers (request id, file name), never passwords or tokens.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stop-and-look-the-debugger\">Stop and look: the debugger\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def total(items):\n    breakpoint()          # opens pdb here (python -m pdb script.py also works)\n    return sum(i[&quot;price&quot;] * i[&quot;qty&quot;] for i in items)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">total\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">items\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">breakpoint\u003c/span>()          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># opens pdb here (python -m pdb script.py also works)\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(i[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;price&quot;\u003c/span>] * i[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;qty&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> items)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>At the \u003ccode>(Pdb)\u003c/code> prompt: \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> next line, \u003ccode>s\u003c/code> step into a call, \u003ccode>c\u003c/code> continue, \u003ccode>p expr\u003c/code> print a value, \u003ccode>l\u003c/code> list code, \u003ccode>w\u003c/code> show the stack, \u003ccode>q\u003c/code> quit. With pytest, \u003ccode>pytest -x --pdb\u003c/code> opens the debugger at the first failing test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-a-script-hangs\">When a script hangs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import faulthandler, signal\nfaulthandler.register(signal.SIGUSR1)       # kill -USR1 PID prints every thread's stack\nfaulthandler.dump_traceback_later(300)      # or dump after 5 minutes\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> faulthandler, signal\nfaulthandler.register(signal.SIGUSR1)       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># kill -USR1 PID prints every thread&#x27;s stack\u003c/span>\nfaulthandler.dump_traceback_later(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">300\u003c/span>)      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># or dump after 5 minutes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"py-spy dump --pid 4321\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>py-spy dump --pid 4321\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Thread 4321 (idle): &quot;MainThread&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    recv (socket.py:707)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    ...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    fetch (sync.py:18)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A stack that ends in \u003ccode>recv\u003c/code> for a long time means the program is waiting on the network. The usual cause is an HTTP call with no timeout. \u003ccode>py-spy\u003c/code> can inspect a running process without changing it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> What does this function return the second time it is called?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>def add(x, items=[]):\n    items.append(x)\n    return items\nadd(1)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Calling add(2) returns [1, 2]. The default list is created once and reused.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Default argument values are evaluated when the function is defined. Use items=None and create a new list inside the function.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shrink-the-problem\">Shrink the problem\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Copy the failing input into a small test. Remove parts until the failure disappears, and the last part removed is the cause. A failing test that you keep is the best record of the bug.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Debugging Python scripts and services fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Read the traceback from the bottom\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Logging beats print\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Stop and look: the debugger\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">When a script hangs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Shrink the problem\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Read the traceback from the bottom to Logging beats print; 2, Logging beats print to Stop and look: the debugger; 3, Stop and look: the debugger to When a script hangs; 4, When a script hangs to Shrink the problem.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Read the traceback from the bottom \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The last line names the error and the value (KeyError: 'qty').\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Debugging with the full data set instead of a small failing example.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Logging beats print \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Levels let you keep debug detail switched off in normal runs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Catching Exception and doing nothing, which hides the real error.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Stop and look: the debugger \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> At the (Pdb) prompt: n next line, s step into a call, c continue, p expr print a value, l list code, w show the stack, q quit.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>When a script hangs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A stack that ends in recv for a long time means the program is waiting on the network.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Mutable default arguments.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Shrink the problem \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Copy the failing input into a small test.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-job-that-never-finished\">Incident: the job that never finished\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly sync usually takes ten minutes. One night it is still running at noon and blocks the next run.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"py-spy dump --pid 18822 | head -8\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>py-spy dump --pid 18822 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Thread 18822 (idle): &quot;MainThread&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    recv (ssl.py:1134)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    read (http/client.py:466)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    send (requests/adapters.py:667)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    get (requests/sessions.py:602)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    fetch_page (sync.py:41)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The dump showed the program stuck reading from a socket in \u003ccode>fetch_page\u003c/code>. The request had no timeout, and the remote server had stopped responding. The fix was \u003ccode>timeout=(3, 15)\u003c/code>, a retry with a limit, and a lock file with a maximum run time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Catching \u003ccode>Exception\u003c/code> and doing nothing, which hides the real error\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Printing secrets while debugging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging with the full data set instead of a small failing example\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mutable default arguments\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"read-the-traceback-from-the-bottom","text":"Read the traceback from the bottom"},{"id":"logging-beats-print","text":"Logging beats print"},{"id":"stop-and-look-the-debugger","text":"Stop and look: the debugger"},{"id":"when-a-script-hangs","text":"When a script hangs"},{"id":"shrink-the-problem","text":"Shrink the problem"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-job-that-never-finished","text":"Incident: the job that never finished"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Python docs: pdb, the debugger","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/pdb.html"},{"t":"Python docs: faulthandler","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/faulthandler.html"}],"text":"Python usually tells you what went wrong, and in which line. Many hours are lost because people read the top of the error instead of the bottom, add print statements everywhere, or cannot see why a script simply hangs. This lesson shows a calm routine for finding the cause. What you will learn Reading a traceback correctly Using logging instead of print Stepping through code with breakpoint() and pdb Finding a script that hangs, with faulthandler and py-spy Reproducing a failure with the smallest possible input Read the traceback from the bottom $ python sync.py Traceback (most recent call last): File \"sync.py\", line 31, in \u003cmodule> main() File \"sync.py\", line 24, in main total += item[\"price\"] * item[\"qty\"] KeyError: 'qty' The last line names the error and the value ( KeyError: &#39;qty&#39; ). The line above it shows where it happened. The frames above that show how the code got there. Here an item has no qty . Decide whether the data is wrong or the code should cope ( item.get(\"qty\", 0) ), and then add a test with that input. Logging beats print import logging logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format = \"%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(name)s %(message)s\" ) log = logging.getLogger( \"sync\" ) log.info( \"fetching %s\" , url) try : resp = session.get(url, timeout= 10 ) resp.raise_for_status() except Exception: log.exception( \"fetch failed for %s\" , url) # includes the traceback raise Levels let you keep debug detail switched off in normal runs. log.exception records the full traceback. Log identifiers (request id, file name), never passwords or tokens. Stop and look: the debugger def total ( items ): breakpoint () # opens pdb here (python -m pdb script.py also works) return sum (i[ \"price\" ] * i[ \"qty\" ] for i in items) At the (Pdb) prompt: n next line, s step into a call, c continue, p expr print a value, l list code, w show the stack, q quit. With pytest, pytest -x --pdb opens the debugger at the first failing test. When a script hangs import faulthandler, signal faulthandler.register(signal.SIGUSR1) # kill -USR1 PID prints every thread&#x27;s stack faulthandler.dump_traceback_later( 300 ) # or dump after 5 minutes $ py-spy dump --pid 4321 Thread 4321 (idle): \"MainThread\" recv (socket.py:707) ... fetch (sync.py:18) A stack that ends in recv for a long time means the program is waiting on the network. The usual cause is an HTTP call with no timeout. py-spy can inspect a running process without changing it. Predict first. What does this function return the second time it is called? def add(x, items=[]): items.append(x) return items add(1) Your guess Answer: Calling add(2) returns [1, 2]. The default list is created once and reused. Default argument values are evaluated when the function is defined. Use items=None and create a new list inside the function. Shrink the problem Copy the failing input into a small test. Remove parts until the failure disappears, and the last part removed is the cause. A failing test that you keep is the best record of the bug. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Debugging Python scripts and services fits together Flow in order: 1, Read the traceback from the bottom to Logging beats print; 2, Logging beats print to Stop and look: the debugger; 3, Stop and look: the debugger to When a script hangs; 4, When a script hangs to Shrink the problem. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Read the traceback from the bottom Step What it does: The last line names the error and the value (KeyError: 'qty'). If it fails or is weak: Debugging with the full data set instead of a small failing example. Logging beats print Observability What it does: Levels let you keep debug detail switched off in normal runs. If it fails or is weak: Catching Exception and doing no"},"python/sre-automation-aws-with-boto3-safely":{"html":"\u003cp>Scripts that talk to a cloud account can delete real things. The difference between a helpful script and an outage is a few habits: listing completely, selecting narrowly, previewing first and running with the least permission needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Create sessions and clients with the right profile, role and region\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use paginators so you never miss resources beyond the first page\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Select by tags and keep a dry-run default\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handle errors from the API cleanly\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sessions-roles-and-regions\">Sessions, roles and regions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Never hard-code keys. Use a named profile or an assumed role, and set the region explicitly, since a script that quietly uses the default region will inspect the wrong place.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import boto3\n\ndef session(profile=None, region=&quot;eu-west-1&quot;):\n    return boto3.Session(profile_name=profile, region_name=region)\n\nec2 = session().client(&quot;ec2&quot;)\nsts = session().client(&quot;sts&quot;)\nprint(sts.get_caller_identity()[&quot;Arn&quot;])\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> boto3\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">session\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">profile=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>, region=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> boto3.Session(profile_name=profile, region_name=region)\n\nec2 = session().client(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ec2&quot;\u003c/span>)\nsts = session().client(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sts&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(sts.get_caller_identity()[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Arn&quot;\u003c/span>])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Print the caller identity at the start of a risky script. It is a cheap guard against running against production by mistake.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"always-paginate\">Always paginate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>API calls return limited pages. A loop that reads only the first response will miss resources and make your report wrong.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def untagged_volumes(ec2):\n    paginator = ec2.get_paginator(&quot;describe_volumes&quot;)\n    for page in paginator.paginate(Filters=[{&quot;Name&quot;: &quot;status&quot;, &quot;Values&quot;: [&quot;available&quot;]}]):\n        for vol in page[&quot;Volumes&quot;]:\n            tags = {t[&quot;Key&quot;]: t[&quot;Value&quot;] for t in vol.get(&quot;Tags&quot;, [])}\n            if &quot;owner&quot; not in tags:\n                yield vol[&quot;VolumeId&quot;], vol[&quot;Size&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">untagged_volumes\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">ec2\u003c/span>):\n    paginator = ec2.get_paginator(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;describe_volumes&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> page \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> paginator.paginate(Filters=[{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Values&quot;\u003c/span>: [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;available&quot;\u003c/span>]}]):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> vol \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> page[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Volumes&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n            tags = {t[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Key&quot;\u003c/span>]: t[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Value&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> t \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> vol.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Tags&quot;\u003c/span>, [])}\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> tags:\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">yield\u003c/span> vol[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;VolumeId&quot;\u003c/span>], vol[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Size&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Server-side filters reduce data; tags decide ownership. Treat a missing tag as &quot;unknown owner&quot;, which is a reason to ask, not a reason to delete.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dry-run-first\">Dry run first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import botocore.exceptions as bx\n\ndef delete_volumes(ec2, ids, apply=False):\n    for vid in ids:\n        try:\n            ec2.delete_volume(VolumeId=vid, DryRun=not apply)\n        except bx.ClientError as err:\n            code = err.response[&quot;Error&quot;][&quot;Code&quot;]\n            if code == &quot;DryRunOperation&quot;:\n                print(&quot;would delete&quot;, vid)\n            else:\n                print(&quot;failed&quot;, vid, code)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> botocore.exceptions \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> bx\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">delete_volumes\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">ec2, ids, apply=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> vid \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> ids:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">try\u003c/span>:\n            ec2.delete_volume(VolumeId=vid, DryRun=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> apply)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> bx.ClientError \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> err:\n            code = err.response[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Error&quot;\u003c/span>][\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Code&quot;\u003c/span>]\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> code == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DryRunOperation&quot;\u003c/span>:\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;would delete&quot;\u003c/span>, vid)\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span>:\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;failed&quot;\u003c/span>, vid, code)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Many EC2 calls support \u003ccode>DryRun\u003c/code>, which returns the error \u003ccode>DryRunOperation\u003c/code> when the call would have succeeded. For services without it, implement your own: print the plan by default and require an explicit \u003ccode>--apply\u003c/code> flag. Catch \u003ccode>ClientError\u003c/code> and look at the error code, because a throttling or permission error needs different handling from a missing resource.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"least-privilege\">Least privilege\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Give automation its own role that can do only the listed actions in the accounts it needs, and no more. A read-only role for reporting scripts and a separate, narrower one for changes makes accidents smaller. Log every change with the caller identity, the resource and the time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A cleanup script lists volumes with one describe_volumes call and finds 1000 results. Is the list complete?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Not necessarily. Responses are paged, so use a paginator or follow the next token until none remains.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Always paginate.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How SRE automation: AWS with boto3, safely fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Sessions, roles and regions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Always paginate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dry run first\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Least privilege\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Sessions, roles and regions to Always paginate; 2, Always paginate to Dry run first; 3, Dry run first to Least privilege.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Sessions, roles and regions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Never hard-code keys. Use a named profile or an assumed role, and set the region explicitly, since a script that quietly uses the default region will inspect the wrong...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Relying on the default region or credentials.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Always paginate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> API calls return limited pages. A loop that reads only the first response will miss resources and make your report wrong.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reading only the first page of results.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Dry run first \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Many EC2 calls support DryRun, which returns the error DryRunOperation when the call would have succeeded.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Deleting on &quot;no owner tag&quot; without asking.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Least privilege \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Give automation its own role that can do only the listed actions in the accounts it needs, and no more.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using one powerful role for everything.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-cleanup-that-found-nothing-and-then-too-much\">Incident: The cleanup that found nothing and then too much\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A script meant to delete unattached volumes reported &quot;0 candidates&quot; in the test account and deleted volumes in production.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python cleanup.py --region eu-west-1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python cleanup.py --region eu-west-1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">caller: arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/ops-admin\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">deleting 14 volumes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The test run had used the default region, which had no volumes. In production the region was set, the role was an admin role, and the script had no dry run. Some volumes were detached snapshots-in-waiting for a migration. The team restored from snapshots, changed the script to print the plan by default, require \u003ccode>--apply\u003c/code>, check an \u003ccode>owner\u003c/code> tag and run with a narrow role, and added the caller identity and region to the first line of output.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading only the first page of results\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relying on the default region or credentials\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting on &quot;no owner tag&quot; without asking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using one powerful role for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"sessions-roles-and-regions","text":"Sessions, roles and regions"},{"id":"always-paginate","text":"Always paginate"},{"id":"dry-run-first","text":"Dry run first"},{"id":"least-privilege","text":"Least privilege"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-cleanup-that-found-nothing-and-then-too-much","text":"Incident: The cleanup that found nothing and then too much"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Boto3: paginators","u":"https://boto3.amazonaws.com/v1/documentation/api/latest/guide/paginators.html"},{"t":"Boto3: credentials","u":"https://boto3.amazonaws.com/v1/documentation/api/latest/guide/credentials.html"},{"t":"AWS IAM best practices","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html"}],"text":"Scripts that talk to a cloud account can delete real things. The difference between a helpful script and an outage is a few habits: listing completely, selecting narrowly, previewing first and running with the least permission needed. What you will learn Create sessions and clients with the right profile, role and region Use paginators so you never miss resources beyond the first page Select by tags and keep a dry-run default Handle errors from the API cleanly Sessions, roles and regions Never hard-code keys. Use a named profile or an assumed role, and set the region explicitly, since a script that quietly uses the default region will inspect the wrong place. import boto3 def session ( profile= None , region= \"eu-west-1\" ): return boto3.Session(profile_name=profile, region_name=region) ec2 = session().client( \"ec2\" ) sts = session().client( \"sts\" ) print (sts.get_caller_identity()[ \"Arn\" ]) Print the caller identity at the start of a risky script. It is a cheap guard against running against production by mistake. Always paginate API calls return limited pages. A loop that reads only the first response will miss resources and make your report wrong. def untagged_volumes ( ec2 ): paginator = ec2.get_paginator( \"describe_volumes\" ) for page in paginator.paginate(Filters=[{ \"Name\" : \"status\" , \"Values\" : [ \"available\" ]}]): for vol in page[ \"Volumes\" ]: tags = {t[ \"Key\" ]: t[ \"Value\" ] for t in vol.get( \"Tags\" , [])} if \"owner\" not in tags: yield vol[ \"VolumeId\" ], vol[ \"Size\" ] Server-side filters reduce data; tags decide ownership. Treat a missing tag as \"unknown owner\", which is a reason to ask, not a reason to delete. Dry run first import botocore.exceptions as bx def delete_volumes ( ec2, ids, apply= False ): for vid in ids: try : ec2.delete_volume(VolumeId=vid, DryRun= not apply) except bx.ClientError as err: code = err.response[ \"Error\" ][ \"Code\" ] if code == \"DryRunOperation\" : print ( \"would delete\" , vid) else : print ( \"failed\" , vid, code) Many EC2 calls support DryRun , which returns the error DryRunOperation when the call would have succeeded. For services without it, implement your own: print the plan by default and require an explicit --apply flag. Catch ClientError and look at the error code, because a throttling or permission error needs different handling from a missing resource. Least privilege Give automation its own role that can do only the listed actions in the accounts it needs, and no more. A read-only role for reporting scripts and a separate, narrower one for changes makes accidents smaller. Log every change with the caller identity, the resource and the time. Predict first. A cleanup script lists volumes with one describe_volumes call and finds 1000 results. Is the list complete? Your guess Answer: Not necessarily. Responses are paged, so use a paginator or follow the next token until none remains. Always paginate. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How SRE automation: AWS with boto3, safely fits together Flow in order: 1, Sessions, roles and regions to Always paginate; 2, Always paginate to Dry run first; 3, Dry run first to Least privilege. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Sessions, roles and regions Step What it does: Never hard-code keys. Use a named profile or an assumed role, and set the region explicitly, since a script that quietly uses the default region will inspect the wrong... If it fails or is weak: Relying on the default region or credentials. Always paginate Step What it does: API calls return limited pages. A loop that reads only the first response will miss resources and make your report wrong. If it fails or is weak: Reading only the first page of results. Dry run first Step What it does: Many EC2 calls support DryRun, which returns the error D"},"python/sre-automation-kubernetes-client-scripts-and-reports":{"html":"\u003cp>\u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code> is perfect for people. For scheduled reports and safe automation, a Python script using the API client gives you structured data, real error handling and tests. This lesson builds a small report you could run daily.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Load cluster configuration inside and outside the cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>List pods and read their status as structured objects\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Find common problems: restarts, missing limits and pending pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep scripts read-only unless a change is explicitly requested\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"connecting\">Connecting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"from kubernetes import client, config\n\ndef api():\n    try:\n        config.load_incluster_config()\n    except config.ConfigException:\n        config.load_kube_config()\n    return client.CoreV1Api()\n\nv1 = api()\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> kubernetes \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> client, config\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">api\u003c/span>():\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">try\u003c/span>:\n        config.load_incluster_config()\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> config.ConfigException:\n        config.load_kube_config()\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> client.CoreV1Api()\n\nv1 = api()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Inside a pod the client uses the service account token. Outside it reads your kubeconfig. Print the current context at start in laptop runs, since a script that changes the wrong cluster is a common accident.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-health-report\">A health report\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def restarts(v1, namespace=None, threshold=5):\n    pods = (v1.list_namespaced_pod(namespace) if namespace\n            else v1.list_pod_for_all_namespaces())\n    for p in pods.items:\n        for cs in (p.status.container_statuses or []):\n            if cs.restart_count &gt;= threshold:\n                reason = cs.last_state.terminated.reason if cs.last_state.terminated else &quot;&quot;\n                yield p.metadata.namespace, p.metadata.name, cs.name, cs.restart_count, reason\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">restarts\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">v1, namespace=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>, threshold=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    pods = (v1.list_namespaced_pod(namespace) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> namespace\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> v1.list_pod_for_all_namespaces())\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> pods.items:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> cs \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> (p.status.container_statuses \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">or\u003c/span> []):\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> cs.restart_count &gt;= threshold:\n                reason = cs.last_state.terminated.reason \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> cs.last_state.terminated \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">yield\u003c/span> p.metadata.namespace, p.metadata.name, cs.name, cs.restart_count, reason\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The objects mirror the API: \u003ccode>metadata\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>spec\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>status\u003c/code>. Container statuses give restart counts and the reason for the last termination, such as \u003ccode>OOMKilled\u003c/code>. For large clusters pass \u003ccode>limit\u003c/code> and follow the \u003ccode>continue\u003c/code> token to page through results instead of loading everything at once.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"policy-checks\">Policy checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def missing_limits(v1):\n    for p in v1.list_pod_for_all_namespaces().items:\n        for c in p.spec.containers:\n            res = c.resources\n            if not res or not res.limits or &quot;memory&quot; not in res.limits:\n                yield p.metadata.namespace, p.metadata.name, c.name\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">missing_limits\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">v1\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> v1.list_pod_for_all_namespaces().items:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> p.spec.containers:\n            res = c.resources\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> res \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">or\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> res.limits \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">or\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;memory&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> res.limits:\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">yield\u003c/span> p.metadata.namespace, p.metadata.name, c.name\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Reports like this find pods without memory limits, images using \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code>, or pods pending for a long time. Write results as CSV or JSON so other tools can use them, and keep a stable sort order so day-to-day diffs are meaningful.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-only-by-default\">Read-only by default\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Give the service account only \u003ccode>get\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>list\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>watch\u003c/code> permissions on the resources it reports on. If a script must change something (for example cordon a node), ask for a flag, log what it changed, and use a separate, narrower role. Handle \u003ccode>ApiException\u003c/code>: a \u003ccode>403\u003c/code> means a permissions gap, a \u003ccode>404\u003c/code> often means someone already deleted the object, and a \u003ccode>429\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>5xx\u003c/code> should be retried with backoff.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A report script uses list_pod_for_all_namespaces on a cluster with 40,000 pods and times out. What helps?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Request pages with the limit parameter and follow the continue token, or narrow by namespace or label selector.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Page large lists.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How SRE automation: Kubernetes client scripts and... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Connecting\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A health report\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy checks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Read-only by default\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Connecting to A health report; 2, A health report to Policy checks; 3, Policy checks to Read-only by default.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Connecting \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Inside a pod the client uses the service account token.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Running with cluster-admin for a read-only report.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>A health report \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The objects mirror the API: metadata, spec, status.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Loading huge lists in one request.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Policy checks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reports like this find pods without memory limits, images using latest, or pods pending for a long time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Policy checks Reports like this find pods without memory limits, images using latest, or pods pending for a long time.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Read-only by default \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Give the service account only get, list and watch permissions on the resources it reports on.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-report-that-missed-the-problem\">Incident: The report that missed the problem\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A daily report said &quot;no restarts above threshold&quot;, yet a service was crash-looping all week.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python report.py --threshold 5\nkubectl get pods -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python report.py --threshold 5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0 containers above threshold\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">checkout-7c9   0/1   CrashLoopBackOff   2 (30s ago)   6m\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The pod was recreated by its controller every few minutes after failing readiness, so the restart count of each new pod stayed low. The report counted restarts per pod, not per workload. The team changed it to also group by owner and report the number of distinct pods created in the last day, and to flag any workload whose pods are not ready. The count of restarts alone was the wrong signal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Parsing \u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code> text output in scripts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running with cluster-admin for a read-only report\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Loading huge lists in one request\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring a symptom at the wrong level (pod instead of workload)\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"connecting","text":"Connecting"},{"id":"a-health-report","text":"A health report"},{"id":"policy-checks","text":"Policy checks"},{"id":"read-only-by-default","text":"Read-only by default"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-report-that-missed-the-problem","text":"Incident: The report that missed the problem"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes client libraries","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/using-api/client-libraries/"},{"t":"Kubernetes API concepts","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/using-api/api-concepts/"},{"t":"Kubernetes RBAC","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/"}],"text":"kubectl is perfect for people. For scheduled reports and safe automation, a Python script using the API client gives you structured data, real error handling and tests. This lesson builds a small report you could run daily. What you will learn Load cluster configuration inside and outside the cluster List pods and read their status as structured objects Find common problems: restarts, missing limits and pending pods Keep scripts read-only unless a change is explicitly requested Connecting from kubernetes import client, config def api (): try : config.load_incluster_config() except config.ConfigException: config.load_kube_config() return client.CoreV1Api() v1 = api() Inside a pod the client uses the service account token. Outside it reads your kubeconfig. Print the current context at start in laptop runs, since a script that changes the wrong cluster is a common accident. A health report def restarts ( v1, namespace= None , threshold= 5 ): pods = (v1.list_namespaced_pod(namespace) if namespace else v1.list_pod_for_all_namespaces()) for p in pods.items: for cs in (p.status.container_statuses or []): if cs.restart_count >= threshold: reason = cs.last_state.terminated.reason if cs.last_state.terminated else \"\" yield p.metadata.namespace, p.metadata.name, cs.name, cs.restart_count, reason The objects mirror the API: metadata , spec , status . Container statuses give restart counts and the reason for the last termination, such as OOMKilled . For large clusters pass limit and follow the continue token to page through results instead of loading everything at once. Policy checks def missing_limits ( v1 ): for p in v1.list_pod_for_all_namespaces().items: for c in p.spec.containers: res = c.resources if not res or not res.limits or \"memory\" not in res.limits: yield p.metadata.namespace, p.metadata.name, c.name Reports like this find pods without memory limits, images using latest , or pods pending for a long time. Write results as CSV or JSON so other tools can use them, and keep a stable sort order so day-to-day diffs are meaningful. Read-only by default Give the service account only get , list and watch permissions on the resources it reports on. If a script must change something (for example cordon a node), ask for a flag, log what it changed, and use a separate, narrower role. Handle ApiException : a 403 means a permissions gap, a 404 often means someone already deleted the object, and a 429 or 5xx should be retried with backoff. Predict first. A report script uses list_pod_for_all_namespaces on a cluster with 40,000 pods and times out. What helps? Your guess Answer: Request pages with the limit parameter and follow the continue token, or narrow by namespace or label selector. Page large lists. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How SRE automation: Kubernetes client scripts and... fits together Flow in order: 1, Connecting to A health report; 2, A health report to Policy checks; 3, Policy checks to Read-only by default. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Connecting Step What it does: Inside a pod the client uses the service account token. If it fails or is weak: Running with cluster-admin for a read-only report. A health report Step What it does: The objects mirror the API: metadata, spec, status. If it fails or is weak: Loading huge lists in one request. Policy checks Observability What it does: Reports like this find pods without memory limits, images using latest, or pods pending for a long time. If it fails or is weak: Policy checks Reports like this find pods without memory limits, images using latest, or pods pending for a long time. Read-only by default Step What it does: Give the service account only get, list and watch permissions on the resources it reports on. If it fail"},"python/sre-automation-retries-backoff-timeouts-and-idempotency":{"html":"\u003cp>Networks fail, APIs throttle and servers restart. A script that calls the network without a timeout can hang forever, and a script that retries blindly can make an outage worse. Good automation is patient, bounded and safe to run twice.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Set timeouts on every network call\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retry only what is safe, with exponential backoff and jitter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Make operations idempotent so a retry cannot double the effect\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Know when to stop and report\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"timeouts-everywhere\">Timeouts everywhere\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most HTTP libraries have no default timeout. Always set one, and separate connect from read time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import requests\n\nresp = requests.get(&quot;https://api.example.com/v1/items&quot;, timeout=(3.05, 10))\nresp.raise_for_status()\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> requests\n\nresp = requests.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://api.example.com/v1/items&quot;\u003c/span>, timeout=(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3.05\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>))\nresp.raise_for_status()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A timeout turns a silent hang into an error you can handle. Choose values from the real latency of the service, not from guesses, and keep an overall deadline for the whole job.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backoff-with-jitter\">Backoff with jitter\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Retry transient failures only: connection errors, timeouts, \u003ccode>429\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>5xx\u003c/code>. Never retry \u003ccode>400\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>401\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>403\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>404\u003c/code> blindly, since the answer will not change. Wait longer after each attempt, and add randomness so many clients do not retry at the same moment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import random, time\n\ndef call_with_retry(fn, attempts=5, base=0.5, cap=20):\n    for n in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            return fn()\n        except (requests.ConnectionError, requests.Timeout) as err:\n            if n == attempts - 1:\n                raise\n            delay = random.uniform(0, min(cap, base * 2 ** n))\n            time.sleep(delay)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> random, time\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">call_with_retry\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">fn, attempts=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>, base=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.5\u003c/span>, cap=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">range\u003c/span>(attempts):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">try\u003c/span>:\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> fn()\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> (requests.ConnectionError, requests.Timeout) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> err:\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> n == attempts - \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>:\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span>\n            delay = random.uniform(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">min\u003c/span>(cap, base * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span> ** n))\n            time.sleep(delay)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is &quot;full jitter&quot;: a random delay between zero and a growing limit. If a server sends a \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code> header, obey it. Put a cap on total attempts and total time, and log each retry with its reason so you can see a pattern.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"idempotency\">Idempotency\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An operation is idempotent when running it twice has the same effect as running it once. &quot;Set replicas to 3&quot; is idempotent. &quot;Add one replica&quot; and &quot;create an order&quot; are not. For non-idempotent calls, send an \u003cstrong>idempotency key\u003c/strong> (many APIs support one), check whether the thing already exists before creating it, or use &quot;create if not exists&quot; semantics. Then a timeout, where you do not know if the first call worked, is safe to retry.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"circuit-breaking-and-giving-up\">Circuit breaking and giving up\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If a dependency is down, hammering it prevents recovery. Stop after a bounded number of failures, wait, and report clearly what was done and what was not. A script that finishes half the work should say which half, so a person or the next run can continue.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A POST that creates a payment times out. Should the script simply retry it?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Only with an idempotency key or a check that the payment does not exist, because the first request may have succeeded.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A timeout does not mean the request failed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How SRE automation: retries, backoff, timeouts and... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Timeouts everywhere\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backoff with jitter\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Idempotency\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Circuit breaking and giving up\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Timeouts everywhere to Backoff with jitter; 2, Backoff with jitter to Idempotency; 3, Idempotency to Circuit breaking and giving up.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Timeouts everywhere \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Most HTTP libraries have no default timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Calls with no timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Backoff with jitter \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Retry transient failures only: connection errors, timeouts, 429 and 5xx.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retrying errors that can never succeed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Idempotency \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An operation is idempotent when running it twice has the same effect as running it once.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retrying non-idempotent writes with no key.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Circuit breaking and giving up \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> If a dependency is down, hammering it prevents recovery.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-retry-storm\">Incident: The retry storm\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A short database failover turned into a 40-minute outage because every client retried immediately and in a tight loop.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -c &quot;retry&quot; /var/log/app/client.log\ngrep &quot;attempt&quot; /var/log/app/client.log | head -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;retry&quot;\u003c/span> /var/log/app/client.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">184220\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;attempt&quot;\u003c/span> /var/log/app/client.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">attempt 1 failed, retrying immediately\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">attempt 2 failed, retrying immediately\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The failover took 20 seconds, but thousands of clients retried without delay and without limit, so the new primary was flooded as soon as it started and fell over again. The team added exponential backoff with jitter and a retry budget, put timeouts on every call, and made the job&#39;s writes idempotent so retries were safe. The next failover passed unnoticed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Calls with no timeout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying immediately or without a limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying errors that can never succeed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying non-idempotent writes with no key\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"timeouts-everywhere","text":"Timeouts everywhere"},{"id":"backoff-with-jitter","text":"Backoff with jitter"},{"id":"idempotency","text":"Idempotency"},{"id":"circuit-breaking-and-giving-up","text":"Circuit breaking and giving up"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-retry-storm","text":"Incident: The retry storm"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Builders' Library: timeouts, retries and backoff with jitter","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/"},{"t":"Requests: timeouts","u":"https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/advanced/#timeouts"},{"t":"Google SRE book: handling overload","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/"}],"text":"Networks fail, APIs throttle and servers restart. A script that calls the network without a timeout can hang forever, and a script that retries blindly can make an outage worse. Good automation is patient, bounded and safe to run twice. What you will learn Set timeouts on every network call Retry only what is safe, with exponential backoff and jitter Make operations idempotent so a retry cannot double the effect Know when to stop and report Timeouts everywhere Most HTTP libraries have no default timeout. Always set one, and separate connect from read time. import requests resp = requests.get( \"https://api.example.com/v1/items\" , timeout=( 3.05 , 10 )) resp.raise_for_status() A timeout turns a silent hang into an error you can handle. Choose values from the real latency of the service, not from guesses, and keep an overall deadline for the whole job. Backoff with jitter Retry transient failures only: connection errors, timeouts, 429 and 5xx . Never retry 400 , 401 , 403 or 404 blindly, since the answer will not change. Wait longer after each attempt, and add randomness so many clients do not retry at the same moment. import random, time def call_with_retry ( fn, attempts= 5 , base= 0.5 , cap= 20 ): for n in range (attempts): try : return fn() except (requests.ConnectionError, requests.Timeout) as err: if n == attempts - 1 : raise delay = random.uniform( 0 , min (cap, base * 2 ** n)) time.sleep(delay) This is \"full jitter\": a random delay between zero and a growing limit. If a server sends a Retry-After header, obey it. Put a cap on total attempts and total time, and log each retry with its reason so you can see a pattern. Idempotency An operation is idempotent when running it twice has the same effect as running it once. \"Set replicas to 3\" is idempotent. \"Add one replica\" and \"create an order\" are not. For non-idempotent calls, send an idempotency key (many APIs support one), check whether the thing already exists before creating it, or use \"create if not exists\" semantics. Then a timeout, where you do not know if the first call worked, is safe to retry. Circuit breaking and giving up If a dependency is down, hammering it prevents recovery. Stop after a bounded number of failures, wait, and report clearly what was done and what was not. A script that finishes half the work should say which half, so a person or the next run can continue. Predict first. A POST that creates a payment times out. Should the script simply retry it? Your guess Answer: Only with an idempotency key or a check that the payment does not exist, because the first request may have succeeded. A timeout does not mean the request failed. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How SRE automation: retries, backoff, timeouts and... fits together Flow in order: 1, Timeouts everywhere to Backoff with jitter; 2, Backoff with jitter to Idempotency; 3, Idempotency to Circuit breaking and giving up. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Timeouts everywhere Step What it does: Most HTTP libraries have no default timeout. If it fails or is weak: Calls with no timeout. Backoff with jitter Step What it does: Retry transient failures only: connection errors, timeouts, 429 and 5xx. If it fails or is weak: Retrying errors that can never succeed. Idempotency Step What it does: An operation is idempotent when running it twice has the same effect as running it once. If it fails or is weak: Retrying non-idempotent writes with no key. Circuit breaking and giving up Step What it does: If a dependency is down, hammering it prevents recovery. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Incident: The retry storm Incident A short database failover turned into a 40-minute outage because every "},"python/sre-automation-parsing-logs-and-building-incident-timelines":{"html":"\u003cp>During an incident the evidence is spread across application logs, load balancer logs, deploy history and alerts. Python can collect it into one timeline in minutes, and the same script can be reused for the next post-mortem.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Parse JSON lines and plain text lines safely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Count and group by status, endpoint or time bucket\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Normalize time zones and merge several sources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stream large files without loading them into memory\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-and-parse-safely\">Read and parse safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import json, re\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\n\nLINE = re.compile(r'^(?P&lt;ts&gt;\\S+) (?P&lt;level&gt;[A-Z]+) (?P&lt;msg&gt;.*)$')\n\ndef parse(line):\n    line = line.strip()\n    if not line:\n        return None\n    if line.startswith(&quot;{&quot;):\n        try:\n            d = json.loads(line)\n            return {&quot;ts&quot;: d.get(&quot;ts&quot;), &quot;level&quot;: d.get(&quot;level&quot;, &quot;&quot;), &quot;msg&quot;: d.get(&quot;msg&quot;, &quot;&quot;)}\n        except json.JSONDecodeError:\n            return None\n    m = LINE.match(line)\n    return m.groupdict() if m else None\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> json, re\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> datetime \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> datetime, timezone\n\nLINE = re.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">compile\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">r&#x27;^(?P&lt;ts&gt;\\S+) (?P&lt;level&gt;[A-Z]+) (?P&lt;msg&gt;.*)$&#x27;\u003c/span>)\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">parse\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">line\u003c/span>):\n    line = line.strip()\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> line:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> line.startswith(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{&quot;\u003c/span>):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">try\u003c/span>:\n            d = json.loads(line)\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ts&quot;\u003c/span>: d.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ts&quot;\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;level&quot;\u003c/span>: d.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;level&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;msg&quot;\u003c/span>: d.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;msg&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>)}\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> json.JSONDecodeError:\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>\n    m = LINE.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">match\u003c/span>(line)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> m.groupdict() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> m \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Logs are messy: partial lines, mixed formats, binary noise. Return \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> for lines you cannot parse and count them, instead of crashing. If many lines fail, the format has changed, which is itself a finding.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"count-and-group\">Count and group\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"from collections import Counter\n\ndef top_errors(path, n=5):\n    c = Counter()\n    with open(path, errors=&quot;replace&quot;) as fh:\n        for line in fh:\n            e = parse(line)\n            if e and e[&quot;level&quot;] == &quot;ERROR&quot;:\n                c[re.sub(r&quot;\\d+&quot;, &quot;N&quot;, e[&quot;msg&quot;])[:80]] += 1\n    return c.most_common(n)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> collections \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> Counter\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">top_errors\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">path, n=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    c = Counter()\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">open\u003c/span>(path, errors=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;replace&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> fh:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> line \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> fh:\n            e = parse(line)\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> e \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span> e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;level&quot;\u003c/span>] == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ERROR&quot;\u003c/span>:\n                c[re.sub(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">r&quot;\\d+&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;N&quot;\u003c/span>, e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;msg&quot;\u003c/span>])[:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>]] += \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> c.most_common(n)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Replace numbers and ids with a placeholder so that similar messages group together. Iterating the file line by line streams it, so memory stays small even for gigabytes. For time buckets, truncate timestamps to the minute and count per bucket to see when the rate changed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"one-timeline\">One timeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def to_utc(text):\n    dt = datetime.fromisoformat(text.replace(&quot;Z&quot;, &quot;+00:00&quot;))\n    return dt.astimezone(timezone.utc)\n\ndef merge(sources):\n    events = []\n    for name, rows in sources.items():\n        for ts, text in rows:\n            events.append((to_utc(ts), name, text))\n    return sorted(events)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">to_utc\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">text\u003c/span>):\n    dt = datetime.fromisoformat(text.replace(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Z&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;+00:00&quot;\u003c/span>))\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> dt.astimezone(timezone.utc)\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">merge\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">sources\u003c/span>):\n    events = []\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> name, rows \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> sources.items():\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> ts, text \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> rows:\n            events.append((to_utc(ts), name, text))\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sorted\u003c/span>(events)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Convert every timestamp to UTC before sorting; mixed time zones are the most common reason timelines look wrong. Combine app errors, load balancer 5xx counts, deploy markers and alert times, and mark the moments of detection, mitigation and recovery. The output (a sorted list or a CSV) is the factual base of the post-mortem.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Two log sources look 5.5 hours apart for the same event. What is the likely cause?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> One source uses local time (for example India) and the other UTC. Normalize both to UTC before merging.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Always compare in one time zone.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How SRE automation: parsing logs and building... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Read and parse safely\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Count and group\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">One timeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Read and parse safely to Count and group; 2, Count and group to One timeline.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Read and parse safely \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Logs are messy: partial lines, mixed formats, binary noise.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Crashing on one malformed line.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Count and group \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Replace numbers and ids with a placeholder so that similar messages group together.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Sorting events with mixed time zones.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>One timeline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Convert every timestamp to UTC before sorting; mixed time zones are the most common reason timelines look wrong.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hand-copying times into a document instead of generating them.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-timeline-that-blamed-the-wrong-release\">Incident: The timeline that blamed the wrong release\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A post-mortem draft said errors started 10 minutes before the deploy, so the team nearly ruled out the release.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"head -1 app.log lb.log\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1 app.log lb.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">==&gt; app.log &lt;==\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-03-04T14:02:11+00:00 ERROR upstream timeout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">==&gt; lb.log &lt;==\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-03-04 19:32:40 status=502\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The load balancer wrote local time (UTC plus 5:30) without an offset. After converting both to UTC, the first 502 came 30 seconds after the deploy finished, not before. The team added a timeline script that requires explicit offsets and marks the source of each event, and the review then focused on the release&#39;s connection pool change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Sorting events with mixed time zones\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Loading a huge file into memory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Crashing on one malformed line\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hand-copying times into a document instead of generating them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"read-and-parse-safely","text":"Read and parse safely"},{"id":"count-and-group","text":"Count and group"},{"id":"one-timeline","text":"One timeline"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-timeline-that-blamed-the-wrong-release","text":"Incident: The timeline that blamed the wrong release"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Python re module","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html"},{"t":"Python collections module","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/collections.html"},{"t":"Python datetime module","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/datetime.html"}],"text":"During an incident the evidence is spread across application logs, load balancer logs, deploy history and alerts. Python can collect it into one timeline in minutes, and the same script can be reused for the next post-mortem. What you will learn Parse JSON lines and plain text lines safely Count and group by status, endpoint or time bucket Normalize time zones and merge several sources Stream large files without loading them into memory Read and parse safely import json, re from datetime import datetime, timezone LINE = re. compile ( r&#x27;^(?P\u003cts>\\S+) (?P\u003clevel>[A-Z]+) (?P\u003cmsg>.*)$&#x27; ) def parse ( line ): line = line.strip() if not line: return None if line.startswith( \"{\" ): try : d = json.loads(line) return { \"ts\" : d.get( \"ts\" ), \"level\" : d.get( \"level\" , \"\" ), \"msg\" : d.get( \"msg\" , \"\" )} except json.JSONDecodeError: return None m = LINE. match (line) return m.groupdict() if m else None Logs are messy: partial lines, mixed formats, binary noise. Return None for lines you cannot parse and count them, instead of crashing. If many lines fail, the format has changed, which is itself a finding. Count and group from collections import Counter def top_errors ( path, n= 5 ): c = Counter() with open (path, errors= \"replace\" ) as fh: for line in fh: e = parse(line) if e and e[ \"level\" ] == \"ERROR\" : c[re.sub( r\"\\d+\" , \"N\" , e[ \"msg\" ])[: 80 ]] += 1 return c.most_common(n) Replace numbers and ids with a placeholder so that similar messages group together. Iterating the file line by line streams it, so memory stays small even for gigabytes. For time buckets, truncate timestamps to the minute and count per bucket to see when the rate changed. One timeline def to_utc ( text ): dt = datetime.fromisoformat(text.replace( \"Z\" , \"+00:00\" )) return dt.astimezone(timezone.utc) def merge ( sources ): events = [] for name, rows in sources.items(): for ts, text in rows: events.append((to_utc(ts), name, text)) return sorted (events) Convert every timestamp to UTC before sorting; mixed time zones are the most common reason timelines look wrong. Combine app errors, load balancer 5xx counts, deploy markers and alert times, and mark the moments of detection, mitigation and recovery. The output (a sorted list or a CSV) is the factual base of the post-mortem. Predict first. Two log sources look 5.5 hours apart for the same event. What is the likely cause? Your guess Answer: One source uses local time (for example India) and the other UTC. Normalize both to UTC before merging. Always compare in one time zone. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How SRE automation: parsing logs and building... fits together Flow in order: 1, Read and parse safely to Count and group; 2, Count and group to One timeline. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Read and parse safely Step What it does: Logs are messy: partial lines, mixed formats, binary noise. If it fails or is weak: Crashing on one malformed line. Count and group Step What it does: Replace numbers and ids with a placeholder so that similar messages group together. If it fails or is weak: Sorting events with mixed time zones. One timeline Step What it does: Convert every timestamp to UTC before sorting; mixed time zones are the most common reason timelines look wrong. If it fails or is weak: Hand-copying times into a document instead of generating them. Incident: The timeline that blamed the wrong release Incident A post-mortem draft said errors started 10 minutes before the deploy, so the team nearly ruled out the release. $ head -1 app.log lb.log ==> app.log \u003c== 2026-03-04T14:02:11+00:00 ERROR upstream timeout ==> lb.log \u003c== 2026-03-04 19:32:40 status=502 The load balancer wrote local time (UTC plus 5:30) without an offset. After converting both to UTC, the first 502 "},"python/sre-automation-chatops-alert-handlers-and-webhooks":{"html":"\u003cp>Many teams connect alerts, chat and automation: an alert posts to a channel, and a button or command runs a runbook step. The idea is simple, but the receiver is an internet-facing program that can run actions, so it needs care.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Receive a webhook and verify that it is genuine\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deduplicate and group alerts so channels stay readable\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Post structured messages with context and links\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep actions behind allow lists, approval and an audit trail\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-minimal-receiver-with-verification\">A minimal receiver with verification\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import hmac, hashlib, json\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer\n\nSECRET = b&quot;read-this-from-a-secret-store&quot;\n\nclass Hook(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    def do_POST(self):\n        size = int(self.headers.get(&quot;Content-Length&quot;, 0))\n        body = self.rfile.read(min(size, 1_000_000))\n        sent = self.headers.get(&quot;X-Signature&quot;, &quot;&quot;)\n        want = hmac.new(SECRET, body, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n        if not hmac.compare_digest(sent, want):\n            self.send_response(401); self.end_headers(); return\n        handle(json.loads(body))\n        self.send_response(202); self.end_headers()\n\ndef handle(event):\n    print(&quot;alert&quot;, event.get(&quot;name&quot;), event.get(&quot;severity&quot;))\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    HTTPServer((&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;, 8080), Hook).serve_forever()\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> hmac, hashlib, json\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> http.server \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer\n\nSECRET = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">b&quot;read-this-from-a-secret-store&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">class\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title class_\">Hook\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">BaseHTTPRequestHandler\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">do_POST\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">self\u003c/span>):\n        size = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">int\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self\u003c/span>.headers.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Content-Length&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>))\n        body = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self\u003c/span>.rfile.read(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">min\u003c/span>(size, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1_000_000\u003c/span>))\n        sent = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self\u003c/span>.headers.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;X-Signature&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>)\n        want = hmac.new(SECRET, body, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> hmac.compare_digest(sent, want):\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self\u003c/span>.send_response(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">401\u003c/span>); \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self\u003c/span>.end_headers(); \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span>\n        handle(json.loads(body))\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self\u003c/span>.send_response(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">202\u003c/span>); \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self\u003c/span>.end_headers()\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">handle\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">event\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;alert&quot;\u003c/span>, event.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>), event.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;severity&quot;\u003c/span>))\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> __name__ == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;__main__&quot;\u003c/span>:\n    HTTPServer((\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>), Hook).serve_forever()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A webhook URL can leak, so never trust the body alone. The sender signs the body with a shared secret, and you recompute the HMAC and compare in constant time with \u003ccode>hmac.compare_digest\u003c/code>. Limit the body size, answer quickly with \u003ccode>202\u003c/code>, and do slow work in a queue or a thread. In production use a real framework behind TLS, with the secret in a secret manager.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-the-channel-readable\">Keep the channel readable\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Send one message per incident, not per alert. Use a \u003cstrong>fingerprint\u003c/strong> (service, alert name and environment) as the key. If it was seen in the last few minutes, update the existing message or increase a counter instead of posting again. Include what matters: severity, service, a one-line symptom, a dashboard link, the runbook link and who is on call. Avoid walls of JSON.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"actions-with-guard-rails\">Actions with guard rails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A button that restarts a service is useful and dangerous. Define an \u003cstrong>allow list\u003c/strong> of actions (restart service X in staging, scale Y between 2 and 10), check who pressed the button against a group, ask for a second approval for production, and write an audit line with person, action, target and result. Make actions idempotent and time-limited, and never let chat text become a shell command: map a fixed action name to a fixed function.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A chat command says &quot;restart $SERVICE&quot; and the script runs os.system(&quot;systemctl restart &quot; + name). What is wrong?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> User text reaches a shell, so a crafted name can run any command. Use an allow list and pass arguments as a list without a shell.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Never build commands from chat input.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How SRE automation: chatops, alert handlers and... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A minimal receiver with verification\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Keep the channel readable\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Actions with guard rails\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, A minimal receiver with verification to Keep the channel readable; 2, Keep the channel readable to Actions with guard rails.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>A minimal receiver with verification \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A webhook URL can leak, so never trust the body alone.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Accepting webhooks without a signature check.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Keep the channel readable \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Send one message per incident, not per alert.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Posting every alert as a separate message.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Actions with guard rails \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A button that restarts a service is useful and dangerous.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Actions without approval or an audit trail.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-channel-nobody-read\">Incident: The channel nobody read\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During a real outage the on-call engineer missed the key alert because the channel had 900 messages in an hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -c &quot;DiskPressure&quot; alerts.log\ngrep -c &quot;PaymentsDown&quot; alerts.log\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DiskPressure&quot;\u003c/span> alerts.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">312\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;PaymentsDown&quot;\u003c/span> alerts.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One flapping node alert posted 312 times and buried the single message that mattered. The team added fingerprint-based grouping with a cooldown, routed severity-high alerts to paging and the rest to a quiet channel, and added a rule that a flapping alert posts once with a counter. The next incident had a short, readable timeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Accepting webhooks without a signature check\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Posting every alert as a separate message\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting chat input reach a shell\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Actions without approval or an audit trail\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-minimal-receiver-with-verification","text":"A minimal receiver with verification"},{"id":"keep-the-channel-readable","text":"Keep the channel readable"},{"id":"actions-with-guard-rails","text":"Actions with guard rails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-channel-nobody-read","text":"Incident: The channel nobody read"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Python hmac module","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/hmac.html"},{"t":"Python http.server module","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/http.server.html"},{"t":"OWASP Top Ten","u":"https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/"}],"text":"Many teams connect alerts, chat and automation: an alert posts to a channel, and a button or command runs a runbook step. The idea is simple, but the receiver is an internet-facing program that can run actions, so it needs care. What you will learn Receive a webhook and verify that it is genuine Deduplicate and group alerts so channels stay readable Post structured messages with context and links Keep actions behind allow lists, approval and an audit trail A minimal receiver with verification import hmac, hashlib, json from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer SECRET = b\"read-this-from-a-secret-store\" class Hook ( BaseHTTPRequestHandler ): def do_POST ( self ): size = int ( self .headers.get( \"Content-Length\" , 0 )) body = self .rfile.read( min (size, 1_000_000 )) sent = self .headers.get( \"X-Signature\" , \"\" ) want = hmac.new(SECRET, body, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest() if not hmac.compare_digest(sent, want): self .send_response( 401 ); self .end_headers(); return handle(json.loads(body)) self .send_response( 202 ); self .end_headers() def handle ( event ): print ( \"alert\" , event.get( \"name\" ), event.get( \"severity\" )) if __name__ == \"__main__\" : HTTPServer(( \"127.0.0.1\" , 8080 ), Hook).serve_forever() A webhook URL can leak, so never trust the body alone. The sender signs the body with a shared secret, and you recompute the HMAC and compare in constant time with hmac.compare_digest . Limit the body size, answer quickly with 202 , and do slow work in a queue or a thread. In production use a real framework behind TLS, with the secret in a secret manager. Keep the channel readable Send one message per incident, not per alert. Use a fingerprint (service, alert name and environment) as the key. If it was seen in the last few minutes, update the existing message or increase a counter instead of posting again. Include what matters: severity, service, a one-line symptom, a dashboard link, the runbook link and who is on call. Avoid walls of JSON. Actions with guard rails A button that restarts a service is useful and dangerous. Define an allow list of actions (restart service X in staging, scale Y between 2 and 10), check who pressed the button against a group, ask for a second approval for production, and write an audit line with person, action, target and result. Make actions idempotent and time-limited, and never let chat text become a shell command: map a fixed action name to a fixed function. Predict first. A chat command says \"restart $SERVICE\" and the script runs os.system(\"systemctl restart \" + name). What is wrong? Your guess Answer: User text reaches a shell, so a crafted name can run any command. Use an allow list and pass arguments as a list without a shell. Never build commands from chat input. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How SRE automation: chatops, alert handlers and... fits together Flow in order: 1, A minimal receiver with verification to Keep the channel readable; 2, Keep the channel readable to Actions with guard rails. Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. A minimal receiver with verification Observability What it does: A webhook URL can leak, so never trust the body alone. If it fails or is weak: Accepting webhooks without a signature check. Keep the channel readable Step What it does: Send one message per incident, not per alert. If it fails or is weak: Posting every alert as a separate message. Actions with guard rails Step What it does: A button that restarts a service is useful and dangerous. If it fails or is weak: Actions without approval or an audit trail. Incident: The channel nobody read Incident During a real outage the on-call engineer missed the key alert because the channel had 900 messages in an hour. $ grep -c \"DiskPressure\" ale"},"python/sre-automation-safe-bulk-operations-and-audit-trails":{"html":"\u003cp>Running a command on one server is a task; running it on 800 servers is an operation. The risks multiply: a mistake repeats everywhere, the target may be overloaded, and half-finished work is hard to describe. Bulk automation needs a small set of safety features built in from the start.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Build a plan and review it before any change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Work in small batches with a pause and a health check\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Respect rate limits and concurrency limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep state so a run can resume, and write an audit trail\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plan-then-apply\">Plan, then apply\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Separate the decision of what to change from the act of changing. The plan step reads current state and produces a list of exact actions. A person (or a check) reviews the list, and only then does the apply step run it. If state changes between plan and apply, re-plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import json, time\n\ndef make_plan(hosts, wanted):\n    return [{&quot;host&quot;: h[&quot;name&quot;], &quot;from&quot;: h[&quot;version&quot;], &quot;to&quot;: wanted}\n            for h in hosts if h[&quot;version&quot;] != wanted]\n\ndef save_plan(plan, path=&quot;plan.json&quot;):\n    with open(path, &quot;w&quot;) as fh:\n        json.dump(plan, fh, indent=2)\n    print(len(plan), &quot;changes planned, review&quot;, path)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> json, time\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">make_plan\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">hosts, wanted\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> [{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;host&quot;\u003c/span>: h[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;from&quot;\u003c/span>: h[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;version&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;to&quot;\u003c/span>: wanted}\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> h \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> hosts \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> h[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;version&quot;\u003c/span>] != wanted]\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">save_plan\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">plan, path=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;plan.json&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">open\u003c/span>(path, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;w&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> fh:\n        json.dump(plan, fh, indent=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(plan), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;changes planned, review&quot;\u003c/span>, path)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Printing the count is a cheap sanity check: a plan that touches 780 of 800 hosts when you expected 12 should stop you.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"batches-limits-and-a-health-gate\">Batches, limits and a health gate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\n\ndef run(plan, apply_one, healthy, batch=10, workers=3, pause=30):\n    done = []\n    for i in range(0, len(plan), batch):\n        chunk = plan[i:i + batch]\n        with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=workers) as pool:\n            results = list(pool.map(apply_one, chunk))\n        done.extend(results)\n        if not healthy():\n            print(&quot;health check failed, stopping after&quot;, len(done))\n            break\n        time.sleep(pause)\n    return done\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> concurrent.futures \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> ThreadPoolExecutor\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">run\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">plan, apply_one, healthy, batch=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>, workers=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>, pause=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    done = []\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">range\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(plan), batch):\n        chunk = plan[i:i + batch]\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=workers) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> pool:\n            results = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">list\u003c/span>(pool.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">map\u003c/span>(apply_one, chunk))\n        done.extend(results)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> healthy():\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;health check failed, stopping after&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(done))\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">break\u003c/span>\n        time.sleep(pause)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> done\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Start with one canary target, then a small batch, then larger ones. Limit workers to what the target systems and APIs can take, and stop on the first failed health check. Add a \u003cstrong>kill switch\u003c/strong>: a file or flag the script checks between batches, so any person can halt the run without killing processes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"resume-and-audit\">Resume and audit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Record each result as it happens, in an append-only file, with time, operator, target, action and outcome. On restart, skip targets already marked done, so a run that died halfway continues without repeating work. Make each action idempotent in case the last one was cut off. After the run, print a summary: planned, succeeded, failed, skipped, with the failures listed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A bulk script stops after 300 of 800 hosts because the laptop lost network. What lets you continue safely?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A saved state file of completed targets and idempotent actions: restart, skip finished hosts and run the rest.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Make long runs resumable.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How SRE automation: safe bulk operations and audit... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Plan, then apply\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Batches, limits and a health gate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Resume and audit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Plan, then apply to Batches, limits and a health gate; 2, Batches, limits and a health gate to Resume and audit.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Plan, then apply \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Separate the decision of what to change from the act of changing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No canary, no health check and no stop button.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Batches, limits and a health gate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Start with one canary target, then a small batch, then larger ones.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No record of who changed what and when.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Resume and audit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Record each result as it happens, in an append-only file, with time, operator, target, action and outcome.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hidden state that makes reruns repeat work.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-change-that-went-everywhere\">Incident: The change that went everywhere\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A script that updated an agent configuration on all hosts applied a bad value to 600 machines in four minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python rollout.py --all --workers 50\nls kill-switch\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python rollout.py --all --workers 50\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">applied 600/800 ... monitoring: errors rising\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> kill-switch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ls: cannot access 'kill-switch': No such file or directory\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>There was no plan review, no canary and no health gate, and 50 workers worked with no pause, so the whole fleet was affected before anyone saw the first alert. Recovery meant a rollback script written during the incident. The team changed the tool to require a plan file, start with one canary, run in batches of 20 with a health check between them and a kill switch, and to log every host change to an audit file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Applying to everything at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No canary, no health check and no stop button\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hidden state that makes reruns repeat work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No record of who changed what and when\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"plan-then-apply","text":"Plan, then apply"},{"id":"batches-limits-and-a-health-gate","text":"Batches, limits and a health gate"},{"id":"resume-and-audit","text":"Resume and audit"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-change-that-went-everywhere","text":"Incident: The change that went everywhere"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Python concurrent.futures","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/concurrent.futures.html"},{"t":"Google SRE workbook: canarying releases","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/"},{"t":"Google SRE book: automation at Google","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/automation-at-google/"}],"text":"Running a command on one server is a task; running it on 800 servers is an operation. The risks multiply: a mistake repeats everywhere, the target may be overloaded, and half-finished work is hard to describe. Bulk automation needs a small set of safety features built in from the start. What you will learn Build a plan and review it before any change Work in small batches with a pause and a health check Respect rate limits and concurrency limits Keep state so a run can resume, and write an audit trail Plan, then apply Separate the decision of what to change from the act of changing. The plan step reads current state and produces a list of exact actions. A person (or a check) reviews the list, and only then does the apply step run it. If state changes between plan and apply, re-plan. import json, time def make_plan ( hosts, wanted ): return [{ \"host\" : h[ \"name\" ], \"from\" : h[ \"version\" ], \"to\" : wanted} for h in hosts if h[ \"version\" ] != wanted] def save_plan ( plan, path= \"plan.json\" ): with open (path, \"w\" ) as fh: json.dump(plan, fh, indent= 2 ) print ( len (plan), \"changes planned, review\" , path) Printing the count is a cheap sanity check: a plan that touches 780 of 800 hosts when you expected 12 should stop you. Batches, limits and a health gate from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor def run ( plan, apply_one, healthy, batch= 10 , workers= 3 , pause= 30 ): done = [] for i in range ( 0 , len (plan), batch): chunk = plan[i:i + batch] with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=workers) as pool: results = list (pool. map (apply_one, chunk)) done.extend(results) if not healthy(): print ( \"health check failed, stopping after\" , len (done)) break time.sleep(pause) return done Start with one canary target, then a small batch, then larger ones. Limit workers to what the target systems and APIs can take, and stop on the first failed health check. Add a kill switch : a file or flag the script checks between batches, so any person can halt the run without killing processes. Resume and audit Record each result as it happens, in an append-only file, with time, operator, target, action and outcome. On restart, skip targets already marked done, so a run that died halfway continues without repeating work. Make each action idempotent in case the last one was cut off. After the run, print a summary: planned, succeeded, failed, skipped, with the failures listed. Predict first. A bulk script stops after 300 of 800 hosts because the laptop lost network. What lets you continue safely? Your guess Answer: A saved state file of completed targets and idempotent actions: restart, skip finished hosts and run the rest. Make long runs resumable. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How SRE automation: safe bulk operations and audit... fits together Flow in order: 1, Plan, then apply to Batches, limits and a health gate; 2, Batches, limits and a health gate to Resume and audit. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Plan, then apply Step What it does: Separate the decision of what to change from the act of changing. If it fails or is weak: No canary, no health check and no stop button. Batches, limits and a health gate Step What it does: Start with one canary target, then a small batch, then larger ones. If it fails or is weak: No record of who changed what and when. Resume and audit Observability What it does: Record each result as it happens, in an append-only file, with time, operator, target, action and outcome. If it fails or is weak: Hidden state that makes reruns repeat work. Incident: The change that went everywhere Incident A script that updated an agent configuration on all hosts applied a bad value to 600 machines in four minutes. $ python rollout.py --all --workers 50 applied 600/800 ... m"}});